-- allium: 3
-- agent-tree.allium
--
-- Scope: The agent file tree panel — a companion pane living inside each
--        agent's own tmux window that shows which files in that agent's
--        worktree have changed.
-- Includes: The git query that answers "what changed" (a diff of the worktree
--           against this branch's fork point from the task's base branch, plus
--           untracked files, plus how many lines each changed file gained and
--           lost); the companion pane's lifecycle (split at agent-window
--           creation, global toggle keybinding bound alongside the board TUI,
--           kill-pane + re-split); the tree/badge rendering rules
--           (worktree-rooted tree, Added/Modified/Deleted badges, line counts,
--           auto-expanded directories); and the DIFF PANE the tree opens
--           beneath itself to show the contents of the change (its open set,
--           its own lifecycle, and its rendering caps); and the AGENTS SECTION
--           beneath the tree, which lists every task with a live agent window
--           and jumps to the selected one's window.
-- Excludes: Task-activity classification and the activity-signal hook (see
--           agent-health.allium — HookPreToolUse fires for both PreToolUse and
--           PostToolUse and is NOT modified by anything in this spec);
--           MCP tool-call auditing and slow-DB warnings (see
--           observability.allium, which deliberately scopes out hook events);
--           the board TUI's own split pane, which joins a whole agent window
--           next to the kanban board (see split-pane.allium — a different pane,
--           in a different window, with a different lifecycle);
--           agent-window creation itself (see dispatch.allium); repository
--           synchronisation and drift measurement, which run their own git
--           commands for a different purpose (see repo-sync.allium).
--
-- GIT IS THE SOLE SOURCE OF TRUTH. The pane runs git against the worktree and
-- renders what git reports; it stores nothing, remembers nothing between
-- refreshes, and derives nothing from the agent's tool calls. Three
-- consequences follow, and they are the whole point of the design:
--   * A deleted file is reported as deleted, because git reports deletions.
--   * A file edited and then reverted stops being badged, because git stops
--     reporting it. Nothing has to retract anything.
--   * A file changed by a shell command, a script or a formatter is badged
--     like any other, because git does not care which tool wrote it.
--
-- An earlier design derived the tree from a hook-driven, append-only log of
-- Read/Write/Edit/NotebookEdit tool calls. It could express none of the three
-- above: the log records touches, never outcomes, so it could not see a
-- deletion, could not retract a badge, and could not see a shell edit at all.
-- That capture pipeline is gone — not dormant — and with it the Read badge,
-- which git cannot supply. See AgentTreeIsGitDerived.
--
-- The pane remains a standalone process inside the agent's own tmux window. It
-- does not touch the board TUI's state, its refresh cycle, or the kanban App.
-- What it needs from the shared rows — its task, and the live agents — it ASKS
-- the running board for, read-only (BoardPaneView); it never opens the store
-- itself (PanesReadThroughTheBoard). The board answers from rows it already
-- holds, so to the board the feature is a reader and nothing more.
--
-- The AGENTS SECTION is the one thing the pane reads that git does not
-- supply. It reads the board's live-agent list, read-only, on its own timer,
-- and never writes to it. That does not bend the git rule: the rule is about
-- what the pane says about FILES, and the agents section says nothing about
-- files. See AgentsSectionListsLiveAgents.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What git says happened to a file, relative to the base. Also the badge
-- vocabulary: a rendered node's badge is a FileChange, so there is exactly one
-- enum for both, and a badge cannot say anything git did not.
--
-- Three values, not more. Git's own porcelain distinguishes further (type
-- changes, copies, unmerged), but the user is scanning for "what did this
-- agent do to my repo", and those all answer it as one of these three.
-- CollapsedGitStatusLetters records the mapping.
enum FileChange { added | modified | deleted }

enum TreeNodeKind { file | directory }

-- Why the diff pane is showing a placeholder for a file instead of its
-- contents. Each value is a fact about the file, not a failure of the pane:
-- the pane rendered exactly what it could, and says which of these it hit.
--
--   untracked -- the file is not in the index, so there is no diff to take.
--                Git's own advice is to stage it, and that is what the pane
--                repeats. See UntrackedFilesHaveNoLineCounts.
--   binary    -- git reports the change without line contents.
--   too_large -- the diff exceeds config.agent_tree_diff_max_bytes.
enum DiffRefusal { untracked | binary | too_large }

-- Which of the two writers set the pane's current notice. The pane shows one
-- notice at a time in one line of border, so the two have to share a field —
-- and a successful refresh must clear a stale "git failed" without also
-- wiping the answer to a keypress the user made half a second ago. See
-- RefreshAgentTree's clearing expression.
--
-- The second writer used to be a failed file open, back when Space and Enter
-- launched an editor. It is now a failed diff-pane split, which is the same
-- shape of event: the direct, immediate answer to a keypress.
--
-- The agents section added two more writers of the same shape. agent_list is
-- the timer's writer, like git_query: a failed read of the task list, cleared
-- by the next successful read. agent_jump is a keypress's writer, like
-- diff_open: a failed jump to another agent's window, which survives every
-- refresh until the next key.
enum NoticeSource { git_query | diff_open | agent_list | agent_jump }

-- Which of the pane's two sections the pane's keys act on. The tree and the
-- agents section each keep their own cursor; focus decides which one a motion
-- moves and what Space and Enter do. See SwitchAgentTreeFocus.
enum AgentTreeFocus { tree | agents }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One changed file, as git reported it on the last successful query. Nothing
-- persists these: they exist for the duration of one refresh pass and are
-- discarded once the nodes are built. There is no log, no schema version and
-- no timestamp, because there is nothing to read back later — the next refresh
-- asks git again.
value GitFileChange {
    path:   String       -- relative to the pane root, exactly as git reports it
    change: FileChange

    -- How many lines this path gained and lost against the baseline, as git
    -- counted them. Null for an UNTRACKED path and only for an untracked path:
    -- a diff against the baseline cannot see a file that is not in the index,
    -- so there is no count to report and none is invented (see
    -- UntrackedFilesHaveNoLineCounts). A tracked path always has both, and
    -- both may be zero — a permission-only change is a real change with no
    -- lines moved.
    --
    -- Null for a BINARY path too, for the same reason rather than a different
    -- one: git reports no line counts for a binary diff, so neither does this.
    --
    -- ONE nullable pair, not two nullable numbers: git either counts a path or
    -- it does not, and there is no case where it reports an addition count
    -- without a removal count. Modelling them as one value makes "half a
    -- count" unrepresentable rather than merely forbidden.
    counts: LineCounts?
}

-- How many lines one path gained and lost against the baseline. Both numbers
-- may be zero — a permission-only change is a real change with no lines moved,
-- and is the reason this is not collapsed into a single signed total.
value LineCounts {
    added:   Integer
    removed: Integer
}

-- The diff of one changed file, as the diff pane shows it. Derived on every
-- refresh from the same baseline the tree is derived from, and persisted
-- nowhere — the same statement AgentTreeIsGitDerived makes about badges, made
-- about contents.
--
-- The body is whatever git printed, verbatim: hunk headers, context and
-- changed lines. It is not parsed, re-indented or re-coloured beyond marking
-- additions and removals, because anything this pane invented about the text
-- would be a second source of truth about the change.
value FileDiff {
    path: String            -- relative to the pane root; matches a GitFileChange.path
    body: String?           -- null exactly when refusal != null
    refusal: DiffRefusal?   -- why there is no body, when there is none

    -- Two nullable fields with a rule joining them (DiffBodyExcludesRefusal),
    -- which is weaker than this spec would like: "neither" is expressible here
    -- and is not a state the pane can draw. An implementation is expected to
    -- collapse the pair into ONE value with two shapes, so the rule holds by
    -- construction rather than by checking — the same reasoning LineCounts
    -- applies to its own pair, and dispatch's does exactly that.
}

-- One row of the rendered tree. Nodes are derived, not stored: they are
-- recomputed from a fresh git query on every refresh, so a node carries no
-- identity of its own. A node exists only for a changed path or an ancestor
-- directory of one — an unchanged file or directory never gets a node, rather
-- than appearing collapsed (see RefreshAgentTree).
--
-- The PANE ROOT is not one of these. It has no row: it is the pane's title,
-- and every node's path is relative to it, so it is not an ancestor directory
-- in the sense above. That is why NoSingleChildDirectoryChains does not reach
-- it — a worktree whose every change is under one directory renders that
-- directory as the single top-level row, and there is nothing above it to
-- merge into.
value AgentTreeNode {
    pane_root: String            -- the pane root this node was rendered under

    -- Path relative to pane_root. A FILE's path spans one component per
    -- directory above it, exactly as git reported it. A DIRECTORY's path may
    -- span SEVERAL components in a single node: a chain of directories that
    -- each hold nothing but the next one is one node, named by the whole
    -- chain — see NoSingleChildDirectoryChains and the surface's
    -- MergedDirectoryChainRows.
    path:      String
    kind:      TreeNodeKind
    badge:     FileChange?       -- null for directories; never null for files
    expanded:  Boolean           -- directories only; always false for files

    -- The line counts shown on this row. For a FILE they are the file's own
    -- (null when it has none — see GitFileChange). For a DIRECTORY they are
    -- the sum over every changed file beneath it, so a collapsed directory
    -- still says how much is inside; see DirectoryCountsSumDescendants.
    --
    -- A directory's sum SKIPS descendants that have no counts rather than
    -- treating them as zero, and is itself null only when no descendant has
    -- any. Counting an unstaged file as "+0 -0" would read as "nothing
    -- changed in there", which is the one thing that is certainly false.
    --
    -- A directory's sum is always DERIVED, whether or not its row draws it.
    -- WHERE it is drawn is a surface decision — see the companion pane's
    -- CountsShowOnTheFolderNearestTheFiles — and this value is what a
    -- collapsed row falls back on when it has to summarise rows the user
    -- cannot see.
    counts: LineCounts?

    -- Whether this file's diff is currently open in the diff pane. Always
    -- false for a directory: a directory is a route to changed files, not a
    -- thing with contents of its own (see OnlyFilesOpenDiffs).
    diff_open: Boolean
}

-- What the board tells a companion pane's renderer about its task. Null in
-- PaneView.task until the board's rows hold the task, which is how "not
-- arrived yet" and "arrived without a worktree" stay two different answers.
value PaneTask {
    worktree: String?     -- the directory the tree is rooted at; null when the task has none
    base_branch: String   -- the NAME the baseline is resolved from (AgentTreeBaselineIsTaskBaseBranch)
}

-- One answer from the board to a pane: its task, when the board's rows hold
-- it, and the tasks with a live agent window (is_live_agent), across every
-- repo and epic, as RefreshAgentTreeAgentList lists them.
value PaneView {
    task: PaneTask?
    live_agents: [core/Task]
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The read a companion pane's renderer makes of the running board, in place of
-- a store connection of its own. The board is found the way a hook finds it
-- (agent-health.allium: HookDelivery). Stated as a contract because the board
-- is another process: it can be down, mid-restart, or a different version.
contract BoardPaneView {
    -- The board's answer for task `task_id`. A board that could not be
    -- reached, refused, or answered in a form this renderer cannot read
    -- gives no PaneView at all: that is a failed read, never an empty view,
    -- so "no agents are running" and "the board did not answer" cannot read
    -- the same.
    pane_view: (task_id: Integer) -> PaneView

    @invariant AskingChangesNothing
        -- Answering writes nothing — not to the store, not to the board's
        -- own rows, not to its display. A pane can ask once a second for as
        -- long as it is shown and the board is exactly as it would have been.

    @invariant AnsweredFromTheBoardsRows
        -- The board answers from the shared rows it already holds, at the
        -- moment it is asked. It opens no connection to answer and does not
        -- wait for a row it does not have yet: a task whose row has not
        -- arrived is answered with PaneView.task = null, and the pane does
        -- the waiting ("Companion Pane Startup").

    @invariant LiveIsTheBoardsOwnDefinition
        -- live_agents holds exactly the tasks is_live_agent holds for in the
        -- board's rows — the Running or Review column with a tmux window
        -- recorded — and no others. The pane does not re-derive it.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The companion pane inside one agent's tmux window. Created by splitting that
-- window at creation time; toggled between shown and hidden by killing and
-- re-splitting the pane. Its state is not persisted — it is re-derived from
-- live tmux, never from a persisted reference.
entity AgentTreePane {
    -- The agent window this pane was split out of. One pane per window
    -- (see OneCompanionPanePerAgentWindow).
    window: core/TmuxWindow

    -- The task this pane renders. Required: a companion pane exists only for a
    -- task-agent window; a window with no task gets no pane at all — see
    -- SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch's "Only task windows get a pane" note.
    --
    -- The task supplies two things the git query needs and nothing else can:
    -- its worktree (pane.root) and its base_branch. The latter is the NAME the
    -- baseline is resolved from, not the baseline itself — see
    -- AgentTreeBaselineIsTaskBaseBranch.
    task: core/Task

    -- Absolute directory the tree is rooted at: always the task's worktree path
    -- (see TaskPaneRootIsTaskWorktree).
    root: String

    status: shown | hidden

    -- The tmux pane exists only while the pane is shown; hiding kills it.
    tmux_pane: core/TmuxPane when status = shown

    -- Stamped on every refresh attempt, successful or not; drives the redraw
    -- timer (see RefreshAgentTree). Stamped even on a failed query so a
    -- repeatedly-failing git cannot turn the refresh trigger into a spin.
    last_rendered_at: Timestamp

    -- The same stamp for the diff pane's own redraw timer
    -- (see RefreshAgentTreeDiff). Separate from last_rendered_at because the
    -- two panes are separate processes on separate clocks: a tree tick that
    -- overran config.agent_tree_git_timeout must not also delay the diff, and
    -- neither pane may stall waiting for the other's git to return.
    last_diff_rendered_at: Timestamp

    -- What the last SUCCESSFUL git query returned. The rendered tree is a
    -- function of this and nothing else, which is what makes "keep the last
    -- good tree" a statement about one field rather than about the renderer's
    -- internals: a failed query leaves it alone (see
    -- AgentTreeGitFailureKeepsLastGoodTree). Empty before the first successful
    -- query, and empty is also the honest answer for a worktree with no
    -- changes yet — both render an empty tree.
    rendered_changes: [GitFileChange]

    -- Which files' diffs the user has opened, relative to root. The ONLY
    -- state in this spec that the user builds up rather than git supplying:
    -- everything else the pane shows is re-derived from a git query every
    -- tick. It is a set, not a cursor — the pane shows every open file's diff
    -- at once, which is what makes ToggleAllAgentTreeFileDiffs a bulk version
    -- of the single-file toggle rather than a separate mode.
    --
    -- It holds PATHS, not nodes, so it survives a refresh that rebuilds every
    -- node. A path whose file stops being reported by git simply stops
    -- matching anything and contributes no diff; it is not an error, and it is
    -- not pruned either (see OpenDiffPathsMaySurviveTheirFiles).
    --
    -- Every path in it lies below pane.root. The tree can only put one there
    -- that it rendered, and it renders none that escape (see changed_tree); the
    -- diff pane checks again on the way in rather than trusting that, because
    -- the two are separate processes reading a file either could have been
    -- handed. A path that fails the check is dropped, not reported: it names
    -- nothing the tree ever showed, so there is nothing to tell the user
    -- about.
    --
    -- Empty is the initial state and the resting state: emptying it is what
    -- closes the diff pane (see CloseAgentTreeDiffPaneWhenEmpty).
    open_diff_paths: [String]

    -- The pane showing those diffs, beneath this one. Exists exactly while
    -- open_diff_paths is non-empty (see DiffPaneExistsWhenSomethingIsOpen).
    -- It lives in the same window and is NOT an AgentTreePane — nothing
    -- renders a tree in it, and the global toggle does not act on it directly.
    diff_pane: core/TmuxPane?

    -- Why the last thing the pane tried to do failed, shown in this pane's own
    -- border until the next keypress. Two things set it: a failed diff-pane
    -- split (see AgentTreeDiffPaneFailureIsVisible) and a failed git query
    -- (see AgentTreeGitFailureKeepsLastGoodTree). The only thing the pane ever
    -- reports to the user, and while it is set the pane's border is drawn in
    -- the error colour (see AgentTreeNoticeRedensBorder).
    error_notice: String?

    -- Which writer set error_notice. Exists only so a recovering git query can
    -- clear its own stale notice without clearing a diff-open notice the user
    -- has had barely a second to read.
    notice_source: NoticeSource when error_notice != null

    -- Which section the pane's keys act on. Starts on the tree, because the
    -- tree is what the pane is for; Tab moves it (SwitchAgentTreeFocus).
    -- Per-process view state like the cursor, and modelled here only because
    -- it decides which rule a Space or Enter reaches.
    focus: AgentTreeFocus

    -- The tasks the agents section shows: what the last SUCCESSFUL read of the
    -- board's task list returned, filtered to tasks with a live agent window
    -- (see is_live_agent) and ordered by task id. A failed read leaves it
    -- alone, exactly as a failed git query leaves rendered_changes alone
    -- (AgentTreeAgentListFailureKeepsLastList). Empty before the first read.
    --
    -- This pane's own task is in it whenever the board says its window is
    -- live, which is almost always. It is listed, marked, and not jumpable —
    -- see JumpToAgentWindow.
    listed_agents: [core/Task]

    -- The agents section's own redraw stamp. Separate from last_rendered_at
    -- because a git query that overran its timeout must not also delay the
    -- task list, nor a slow answer from the board delay the tree.
    last_agents_listed_at: Timestamp

    -- Whether the tree's renderer has read its task back from the board
    -- yet. pane.task is what the pane is FOR; this is whether the
    -- process drawing it has found out. A renderer starts waiting, and
    -- everything else it does — git, the agents section, every key but the
    -- exit key — waits with it. See "Companion Pane Startup" below for why a
    -- task that certainly exists can be unreadable for a moment.
    task_resolution: waiting | resolved | unresolved

    -- When the tree's current renderer process started. The startup wait
    -- (config.agent_tree_startup_wait) is measured from here, per renderer:
    -- a show after a hide is a new process and gets a fresh wait.
    renderer_started_at: Timestamp

    -- What the tree's renderer draws instead of a tree when it gave up
    -- resolving its task. Deliberately NOT error_notice: that field is
    -- cleared by the next keypress (ClearAgentTreeErrorNotice), and a pane
    -- with no task has nothing to show once the text is gone — so this one
    -- stays until the renderer exits.
    startup_failure: String when task_resolution = unresolved

    -- The same three fields for the diff pane's renderer, which is a
    -- separate process that resolves the same task separately, on its own
    -- clock — see RefreshAgentTreeDiff's "The same baseline, asked the same
    -- way" for why the two processes do not hand each other values.
    diff_task_resolution: waiting | resolved | unresolved
    diff_renderer_started_at: Timestamp
    diff_startup_failure: String when diff_task_resolution = unresolved

    -- The task row has become readable while a renderer is waiting for it.
    -- board_task(task) is the renderer's own read of the task from the board
    -- (BoardPaneView): null until the row has arrived in the board's rows,
    -- and null while the board cannot be reached; the task once it has.
    -- The waiting term is part of the condition, not only a guard on the
    -- rules that use it, so a renderer that starts waiting on a row already
    -- present makes the condition turn true afresh. The diff form also
    -- requires a diff pane, since no diff renderer is waiting until one has
    -- been split; its appearance is what turns the condition true.
    task_arrived: task_resolution = waiting and board_task(task) != null
    diff_task_arrived: diff_pane != null and diff_task_resolution = waiting and board_task(task) != null

    -- A renderer either finds its task or gives up; a new renderer (a show,
    -- a re-split) starts waiting again whatever the last one reached.
    transitions task_resolution {
        waiting -> resolved
        waiting -> unresolved
        resolved -> waiting
        unresolved -> waiting
    }

    transitions diff_task_resolution {
        waiting -> resolved
        waiting -> unresolved
        resolved -> waiting
        unresolved -> waiting
    }

    -- Declared last: allium check (3.16) reads only an entity's final
    -- transitions block as the graph for `status`, and reports every
    -- shown/hidden edge as undeclared when this one comes first.
    transitions status {
        shown -> hidden
        hidden -> shown
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Width of the companion pane as a percentage of the agent window.
    -- Deliberately narrower than the board's own 40% split-pane: Claude's CLI
    -- output needs the room, and a file tree is mostly short path segments.
    agent_tree_pane_percent: Integer = 30

    -- Redraw cadence. Each tick runs the git query (see AgentTreeGitQuery) and
    -- rebuilds only if its answer changed, so the visible cost of a one-second
    -- poll is five short-lived git processes per open pane — six when the
    -- baseline resolution has two candidates to rank. That is well below the
    -- point where a follow-along view feels laggy: the added processes are
    -- merge-base probes, which read refs and objects and nothing else, and
    -- git's own index caching makes a repeated no-change query cheap. Not tied
    -- to the board TUI's tick_interval — the two processes are independent.
    agent_tree_refresh_interval: Duration = 1.second

    -- How long a single git COMMAND may run before it is killed and treated as
    -- a failure. A pane must never wedge on a git that is blocked on an index
    -- lock the agent itself holds; the refresh timer would then stop ticking
    -- and the pane would freeze rather than merely go stale.
    --
    -- Deliberately far below the 2-minute timeout the board's other git calls
    -- use. Those run on a worker while a TUI stays live; these run inline in
    -- the renderer's own single-threaded loop, so this timeout bounds how long
    -- the pane can ignore a keypress. Five seconds is generous for a name-only
    -- diff and short enough that a wedged git reads as a hiccup.
    --
    -- The bound is per command and a tick runs up to six of them, so the
    -- arithmetic worst case is six times this. Only three of the six can
    -- realistically reach it: an index lock is by far the commonest cause of a
    -- slow query, and only the two diffs and the untracked listing touch the
    -- index.
    -- The baseline resolution's merge-base probes take no lock, so they do not
    -- move the practical ceiling.
    agent_tree_git_timeout: Duration = 5.seconds

    -- The tmux key (pressed after the tmux prefix) that toggles the companion
    -- pane. "e" for explorer: unbound in tmux's default key table, so the
    -- binding shadows nothing. See the ToggleKeyChoice open question.
    agent_tree_toggle_key: String = "e"

    -- Height of the diff pane as a percentage of the COMPANION PANE'S COLUMN,
    -- not of the agent window. The diff pane subdivides the tree's own 30%
    -- column rather than spanning the window, so the agent's own pane keeps
    -- every column it had and is untouched by opening a diff.
    --
    -- Deliberately the larger share of that column: the tree is a list of
    -- short path segments and stays legible in a few rows, while reading the
    -- change is the point of opening it. The predecessor of this value gave
    -- the editor pane 60% of the whole WINDOW's height, which took the room
    -- from the agent instead.
    agent_tree_diff_pane_percent: Integer = 66

    -- How much diff text the pane will render for ONE file before refusing it
    -- and showing a placeholder instead (DiffRefusal.too_large). The cap is
    -- per file, not per pane: one enormous generated file must not cost the
    -- user the diffs of the files either side of it, which is exactly what a
    -- whole-pane budget would do depending on sort order.
    --
    -- Bounded for the same reason config.agent_tree_git_timeout is: the
    -- rendering runs inline in a single-threaded loop that also has to answer
    -- keypresses, so an unbounded diff is an unbounded freeze.
    agent_tree_diff_max_bytes: Integer = 1048576

    -- How often the agents section re-reads the board's task list. The same
    -- cadence as the tree: agents start and finish on the scale of minutes,
    -- and one small read-only query a second costs nothing a user would see.
    agent_tree_agents_refresh_interval: Duration = 1.second

    -- The most rows the agents section takes from the pane. It grows with the
    -- number of live agents up to this cap and scrolls beyond it; the tree
    -- keeps every row the section does not take. Small on purpose: the tree
    -- is what the pane is for, and eight agents at once is already a busy
    -- board.
    agent_tree_agents_max_rows: Integer = 8

    -- How long a pane's renderer waits, at startup, for the board to answer
    -- with its task's row before giving up and saying so. The row normally
    -- reaches the board well under a second after dispatch (see "Companion
    -- Pane Startup"); ten seconds covers a slow store, or a board mid-restart,
    -- without leaving a user staring at a waiting line for long when the task
    -- really is not there.
    agent_tree_startup_wait: Duration = 10.seconds
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Companion Pane Lifecycle ==
--
-- The toggle is a GLOBAL tmux keybinding, so like split-pane.allium's
-- prefix+space jump-back binding its lifetime is the board TUI process's
-- lifetime: bound when the board starts, unbound when it exits. A companion
-- pane created by an agent window survives the board's exit — only the ability
-- to toggle it goes away.

rule BindAgentTreeToggle {
    when: AppStarts()

    ensures: AgentTreeToggleBound(key: config.agent_tree_toggle_key)

    @guidance
        -- Registered as a prefixed tmux binding alongside the existing
        -- prefix+space jump-back binding (see split-pane.allium:
        -- JumpBackToTuiWindow), through the same bind_key helper and the same
        -- board-startup path. Best-effort: a failing tmux call must not
        -- prevent the board TUI from starting.
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- setup_tmux_for_tui (src/runtime/mod.rs) binds
        -- config.agent_tree_toggle_key right after the existing prefix+space
        -- bind_key call, same function, same best-effort `let _ =` style.
        --
        -- The bound command is a new pattern for this codebase: a
        -- format-string-parameterized run-shell, not a static tmux command
        -- like prefix+space's `select-window -t =TUI`. tmux itself expands
        -- #{window_name} into the command line at the moment the key is
        -- pressed, before invoking the shell — so the process this spawns
        -- (`dispatch toggle-agent-tree-pane <window>`) is handed the name of
        -- whichever window was focused, without having to ask tmux itself.
        -- The `-b` flag backgrounds the shell job so the keypress does not
        -- block the tmux client while it runs. See ToggleAgentTreePane's own
        -- guidance for what that process does with the window name.
        --
        -- Resolves ToggleKeyChoice: config.agent_tree_toggle_key's default
        -- ("e") was adopted as-is, unchanged from the design's own rationale
        -- (unbound in tmux's default key table, no collision with
        -- prefix+space).
        --
        -- Surfaced in the in-TUI help popup ([?], General section) alongside
        -- the jump-back binding — otherwise nothing in the TUI hints that a
        -- companion pane can be toggled at all.
}

rule UnbindAgentTreeToggle {
    when: AppExits()

    ensures: AgentTreeToggleUnbound(key: config.agent_tree_toggle_key)

    @guidance
        -- Torn down with the rest of the board's tmux setup, so the key is
        -- returned to whatever tmux had bound it to. Consequence, shared with
        -- prefix+space: the companion pane cannot be toggled while the board
        -- TUI is not running.
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- teardown_tmux_for_tui (src/runtime/mod.rs) unbinds
        -- config.agent_tree_toggle_key alongside the existing prefix+space
        -- unbind_key call.
}

rule SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch {
    -- Every agent window gets a companion pane the moment it is created, and
    -- only an agent window does. One rule covers every launch path because they
    -- all emit AgentLaunched: fresh dispatch, research dispatch, quick dispatch,
    -- epic auto-dispatch, resume and retry-resume.
    when: AgentLaunched(task, mode)

    requires: task.tmux_window != null
    requires: task.worktree != null

    ensures: AgentTreePane.created(
        window: task.tmux_window,
        task: task,
        root: task.worktree.path,
        status: shown,
        tmux_pane: core/TmuxPane.created(),
        last_rendered_at: now,
        last_agents_listed_at: now - config.agent_tree_agents_refresh_interval,
        focus: tree,
        listed_agents: [],
        -- The renderer starts before its task is necessarily readable; see
        -- "Companion Pane Startup".
        task_resolution: waiting,
        renderer_started_at: now,
        -- No diff pane yet. The first split starts its wait
        -- (SplitAgentTreeDiffPane); these values are never read before then.
        diff_task_resolution: waiting,
        diff_renderer_started_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- The window is split immediately after it is created and the agent
        -- command has been sent, using a split helper that takes both a size
        -- (config.agent_tree_pane_percent) and a command to run in the new
        -- pane — the same "create and immediately run a command" shape the
        -- window-creation helper already establishes. The board's existing
        -- 40%-hardcoded horizontal split helper is left alone; it serves the
        -- board's own split-pane feature (see split-pane.allium).
        --
        -- The new pane runs a standalone tree renderer for this task
        -- (`dispatch agent-tree <task_id>`), not part of the board's App. It
        -- opens on a worktree the agent has not changed yet, in which case the
        -- tree it shows is empty: only changed paths and their ancestors are
        -- rendered, so a pane opened before the agent's first edit shows
        -- nothing but the root (see RefreshAgentTree).
        --
        -- NO RETROFIT. Creation is triggered only by window creation, so agent
        -- windows that already existed when this feature shipped never grow a
        -- pane on their own automatically. A manual toggle can still create
        -- one on demand in such a window — see ShowAgentTreePane's
        -- "Resolves ToggleTargetResolution" note, which is a different
        -- trigger (a deliberate keypress) than the automatic creation this
        -- rule covers.
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- The size-and-command split helper described above is
        -- tmux::split_window_horizontal_running (src/tmux.rs), a sibling of the
        -- board's 40%-hardcoded split_window_horizontal, which is untouched.
        -- It passes the task's worktree as the new pane's start directory
        -- (`split-window -c`), which is what keeps the companion pane out of the
        -- after-split-window correction hook — see split-pane.allium's
        -- AgentWindowSplitStartsInTaskWorktree. The toggle and resync paths hold
        -- only a window name, so they read the worktree back from that window's
        -- @dispatch_dir option.
        -- spawn_agent_tree_pane (src/dispatch/agents.rs) calls it at
        -- config.agent_tree_pane_percent and is wired into dispatch_with_prompt
        -- — covering fresh dispatch, research dispatch and quick dispatch, and
        -- through them epic auto-dispatch — and into resume_agent, covering
        -- resume and retry-resume. In both cases the call sits immediately
        -- after the existing send-keys of the agent command, which is the
        -- "after the agent command has been sent" timing above.
        --
        -- The new pane opens on the LEFT of the agent's own pane (tmux
        -- split-window's -b/"before" flag), keeping focus (-d) on the agent's
        -- pane to its right. Unlike the board's own split_window_horizontal
        -- (no -b, right pane, used by split-pane.allium), the tree reads
        -- left-to-right the way a file tree conventionally does, with the
        -- agent's CLI output remaining the wider, focused pane.
        --
        -- Soft-fail, deliberately: a split that fails is logged at warn level
        -- and the dispatch or resume call still succeeds. No agent fails to
        -- launch because its companion pane could not open — consistent with
        -- BindAgentTreeToggle's best-effort binding.
        --
        -- Only task windows get a pane: AgentLaunched is the only trigger that
        -- ever creates a companion pane, and AgentTreePane.task is required
        -- rather than optional. A window with no task has no worktree to root a
        -- tree at and no base branch to diff against; `dispatch agent-tree`
        -- takes a REQUIRED task id (src/cli/agent_tree.rs), so there is no id to
        -- launch it with in the first place. Such a pane would also be covered
        -- by neither pane-teardown rule below, since both key off a Task.
        --
        -- This is now structural rather than test-enforced, and deliberately
        -- so. It used to be asserted against real tmux, because dispatch had a
        -- second window-creation path that launched an agent without a task
        -- (the main session, since removed) and that path could have grown a
        -- split by accident. With it gone, AgentLaunched is the only trigger
        -- that creates a pane and it cannot fire without a Task, so a
        -- real-tmux test would assert something no code path can reach. What
        -- remains covered is the toggle: toggle_agent_tree_pane short-circuits
        -- on a name that is not `task-<id>`, asserted at mock level by
        -- toggle_agent_tree_pane_is_noop_for_non_task_window (src/dispatch/tests/agent_launch.rs)
        -- and resync_agent_tree_pane_noop_for_non_task_window
        -- (src/dispatch/agents.rs). A future third window-creation path that
        -- launches an agent would put the real-tmux assertion back in scope.
}

rule HideAgentTreePane {
    when: ToggleAgentTreePane(user)

    let pane = AgentTreePane{window: current_tmux_window()}

    requires: exists pane
    requires: pane.status = shown

    ensures: pane.status = hidden
    ensures: pane.tmux_pane = null

    @guidance
        -- Hiding kills the pane outright rather than zooming or resizing it,
        -- matching the board's own enter/exit split mechanism (kill-pane, then
        -- re-split on the way back). The cost is that the renderer process dies
        -- and restarts on the next show; that is cheap, because all its state
        -- comes from one git query it can just run again.
        --
        -- == The diff pane IS hidden with it ==
        -- Both panes go, and the open set goes with them — see
        -- KillAgentTreeDiffPaneWithItsTree, which fires on the same transition
        -- this rule performs. Nothing drives a diff pane whose tree is gone: no
        -- process refreshes it, no keypress can change what it shows, and the
        -- toggle that brings the tree back does not act on it.
        --
        -- The predecessor of this paragraph said the opposite, of the editor
        -- pane this replaced, and it was right about that one: an editor
        -- holding a file the user asked for is useful with or without a tree
        -- beside it, and it could be reused by a later renderer because it was
        -- found by a mark rather than by anything a renderer held. A diff pane
        -- has neither property — it is a view of a set only the tree can
        -- change — so it is killed rather than orphaned.
        --
        -- The killed pane is the companion pane, never the agent's own pane:
        -- the target is resolved from the AgentTreePane record for the focused
        -- window, not from "whichever pane is not focused".
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- No AgentTreePane record is persisted anywhere (no DB row, no tmux
        -- user option) — `let pane = AgentTreePane{window: current_tmux_window()}`
        -- above is a spec-level abstraction, not a stored lookup. The
        -- implementation re-derives both halves of it live from tmux on every
        -- toggle: `current_tmux_window()` is resolved by tmux's own
        -- #{window_name} format-string expansion inside the bound run-shell
        -- command (see BindAgentTreeToggle), before dispatch even starts —
        -- this process is simply handed the window name as an argv string.
        -- `pane.status = shown` means the window HAS a companion pane, which is
        -- read by identifying that pane directly (below), not from any stored
        -- status field.
        --
        -- == How the companion pane is identified ==
        -- By a role marker dispatch writes on the pane at the moment it creates
        -- it: a pane-scoped tmux user option whose value says what the pane is
        -- for. The companion tree pane carries the agent-tree role; the pane
        -- SplitAgentTreeDiffPane opens carries the diff role. The lookup
        -- matches the role's EXACT value, so the two never stand in for each
        -- other — a toggle cannot kill the diff pane, and opening a diff
        -- cannot respawn the tree. No pane in the window carrying the
        -- agent-tree role means hidden.
        --
        -- One marker, both panes, and that is the point: "a pane dispatch put in
        -- this window" is now exactly "a pane carrying this option", whatever
        -- its role — which is what the pin drain reads (see
        -- ToggleVsSplitPaneInteraction below and split-pane.allium's
        -- PinTaskInSplitPane), in one lookup, and without being taught about any
        -- future kind of dispatch-created pane.
        --
        -- == What this replaced, part 1: the start command ==
        -- Identity was first derived from the command the pane was started with
        -- (tmux's #{pane_start_command}), matched as argv0's basename plus argv1.
        -- It was correct, and it was chosen because it needed no migration — but
        -- it re-derived identity from a string tmux was never asked to keep
        -- stable. It whitespace-split a command line, so a dispatch binary at a
        -- path containing a space mis-parsed; it compared argv0 by basename
        -- because that path may be absolute; and it had to actively defend
        -- against substring matching, because a pane showing this very file has
        -- "agent-tree" in its command line. A marker written by the code that
        -- created the pane needs none of those defences: it cannot be confused
        -- by the contents of a file some other pane happens to be displaying.
        --
        -- ACCEPTED COST: the marker is NOT retroactive. The start command was
        -- reported for panes already running; an option can only be written to a
        -- pane being created. So agent windows already open when this changed
        -- carry unmarked panes, and for the remaining life of each such window
        -- the toggle reads its tree as hidden and splits a SECOND tree pane, and
        -- pinning or resyncing no longer finds those panes. This was accepted
        -- deliberately (#3902): the regression is one-off and self-clearing as
        -- agent windows turn over, and each affected window has its own escape
        -- hatch — the second toggle press kills the marked pane the first one
        -- created, and every pane in a fresh window is marked. The two
        -- alternatives were rejected for the same reason: both keep the string
        -- matcher alive, either as a fallback nothing forces anyone to delete, or
        -- inside new startup code that backfills markers — which moves the
        -- fragility rather than removing it.
        --
        -- == What this replaced, part 2: focus ==
        -- Before either form of marker, the kill target used to be "the window's single INACTIVE pane"
        -- (`tmux::inactive_pane_id`), justified by every split helper passing
        -- `-d`, which leaves focus on the agent's own pane at split time. That
        -- is a property of the split, not of the window, and it failed in two
        -- ways once the user was involved:
        --   * With focus in the companion pane — which the user must take to
        --     press any key in it — the single inactive pane is the AGENT'S, so
        --     the toggle killed the live claude session.
        --   * With a second dispatch-created pane open beside the tree, two
        --     panes are inactive, which read as "hidden", so the toggle split a
        --     SECOND companion pane.
        -- Identifying the pane by what it is rather than by where focus happens
        -- to be is what makes "the companion pane, never the agent's own pane"
        -- true rather than merely usually true. An index-based target remains
        -- wrong for the separate reason it always was: `pane-base-index` shifts
        -- indices and a `-b` split renumbers them.
        --
        -- == RESOLVED: ToggleVsSplitPaneInteraction ==
        -- The former open question here asked whether the board's
        -- split-pane join/swap operations (split-pane.allium: `join_pane`,
        -- `exec_swap_split_pane`) needed to become companion-pane-aware,
        -- since both predate every agent window carrying a companion pane.
        -- Both sides needed fixing, and the first answer recorded here got the
        -- division wrong. On the mutation side: `join_pane` drains the
        -- companion panes left behind when it moves the agent's own pane out
        -- (see split-pane.allium's PinTaskInSplitPane guidance) — every pane
        -- dispatch put in that window, the diff pane included, since with one
        -- open the old single-inactive-pane lookup was ambiguous and orphaned
        -- both. `exec_swap_split_pane` resyncs a renamed window's companion
        -- pane to the task the new name implies (see SwapSplitPane's guidance).
        -- But this rule's own state-reading was NOT "already correct" as
        -- previously claimed: see "What this replaced" above.
        --
        -- A window name that doesn't parse as `task-<id>` (the inverse of
        -- TmuxWindow::for_task — the board's own TUI window, an editor
        -- window, anything else) never reaches the `exists pane` check at
        -- all: toggle_agent_tree_pane
        -- (src/dispatch/agents.rs) short-circuits to a no-op before issuing
        -- any tmux call, which stands in for "not exists pane" without a
        -- record to test.
        --
        -- An argv string that is not a window NAME at all is a third no-op,
        -- one step earlier still. The keybinding hands this process whatever
        -- tmux expanded #{window_name} into, so that argument is parsed at the
        -- border (cmd_toggle_agent_tree_pane in src/main.rs); the empty string
        -- and a pane id are not window names (see core.allium: TmuxWindow), and
        -- for either the process logs and returns without issuing any tmux
        -- call.
}

rule ShowAgentTreePane {
    when: ToggleAgentTreePane(user)

    let pane = AgentTreePane{window: current_tmux_window()}

    requires: exists pane
    requires: pane.status = hidden

    ensures: pane.status = shown
    ensures: pane.tmux_pane = core/TmuxPane.created()
    ensures: pane.last_rendered_at = now
    -- A fresh renderer starts on the tree, like a fresh cursor starts at the
    -- top: focus is view state and does not survive a hide.
    ensures: pane.focus = tree

    -- A fresh renderer also starts with an empty agents list and reads the
    -- board straight away, rather than one interval after it appears.
    ensures: pane.listed_agents = []
    ensures: pane.last_agents_listed_at = now - config.agent_tree_agents_refresh_interval

    -- A fresh renderer is a fresh process, so it resolves its task afresh
    -- and gets its own startup wait, whatever the last renderer reached.
    ensures: pane.task_resolution = waiting
    ensures: pane.renderer_started_at = now
    ensures: pane.startup_failure = null

    @guidance
        -- Re-splits the window at config.agent_tree_pane_percent and restarts
        -- the renderer, which rebuilds the full tree from a fresh git query —
        -- so a hide/show cycle loses nothing but the scroll position and any
        -- manual expand/collapse the user had done. Being stateless is what
        -- makes this true: there is no history to lose.
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- `pane.status = hidden` above is read the same live way
        -- HideAgentTreePane's guidance describes: no pane in the window carries
        -- the agent-tree role marker. The re-split reuses
        -- SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch's own
        -- implementation directly — toggle_agent_tree_pane
        -- (src/dispatch/agents.rs) calls the same spawn_agent_tree_pane
        -- helper that agent-launch time uses, so the show side of the toggle
        -- and the initial split are one code path, not two. The task id it
        -- passes to `dispatch agent-tree <task_id>` comes from parsing the
        -- window name (`task-<id>`), never from a stored pane record.
        --
        -- Resolves ToggleTargetResolution in favour of retrofitting: because
        -- "hidden" and "never had a pane" are the same live tmux state (no pane
        -- carrying the marker), a task-<id> window that predates this feature —
        -- or one whose automatic split at launch failed, or one whose pane
        -- predates the role marker itself (see HideAgentTreePane's ACCEPTED
        -- COST) — gets a marked companion pane the first time a user manually
        -- toggles it. That is also what bounds that accepted regression: the
        -- unmarked pane survives one extra press, and every pane the toggle
        -- itself creates is marked. This does not violate
        -- SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch's "no retrofit" guidance, which is
        -- about automatic creation on launch; a deliberate user keypress is a
        -- different trigger.
}

rule DiscardAgentTreePaneOnWindowGone {
    -- The companion pane lives inside the agent window. When that window dies,
    -- the pane dies with it, whether or not dispatch asked for it.
    when: WindowGone(task)

    let pane = AgentTreePane{task: task}

    requires: exists pane

    ensures: not exists pane

    @guidance
        -- Covers the crash path specifically: agent-health.allium's
        -- DetectCrashedAgent deliberately leaves task.tmux_window set so the
        -- retry prompt can still name the window, so the field-transition rule
        -- below would never fire for a crashed agent. The pane record must go
        -- anyway — the tmux pane it refers to no longer exists.
}

rule DiscardAgentTreePaneOnWindowCleared {
    -- Covers every lifecycle path that clears a task's window: wrap-up,
    -- PR merge, manual Review -> Done, delete, and the kill half of a
    -- retry-resume.
    when: task: core/Task.tmux_window transitions_to null

    let pane = AgentTreePane{task: task}

    requires: exists pane

    ensures: not exists pane

    @guidance
        -- Mirrors the trigger split-pane.allium's
        -- SplitPaneRespawnOnWindowCleared uses, for the same reason: keying on
        -- the field transition rather than on each individual lifecycle event
        -- means no path can be forgotten. Unlike the board's split pane, there
        -- is nothing to respawn — the pane is inside the window being
        -- destroyed, so it is simply gone. A retry-resume produces a fresh
        -- pane via SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch.
}

-- == Companion Pane Startup ==
--
-- Each pane's renderer (`dispatch agent-tree <task_id>` for the tree,
-- `dispatch agent-diff <task_id>` for the diff pane) is handed only a task id.
-- Before it can run git it has to read that task back, for the two things only
-- the task knows: its worktree and its base branch. It asks the running board
-- (BoardPaneView), never the store (PanesReadThroughTheBoard).
--
-- THE RACE. The renderer starts within moments of the task being dispatched,
-- and the board's rows may not hold the task yet: a dispatch made on another
-- board, or a row the store has not yet delivered back, arrives a short while
-- later. A renderer that read once and treated "no such task" as final exited
-- on the spot, and since tmux closes a pane whose command exits, the pane
-- vanished the instant it opened, taking its error message with it. Nothing
-- on screen said what had happened.
--
-- So a renderer waits for its task instead of reading it once: until the
-- board answers with the row, for at most config.agent_tree_startup_wait,
-- with a line saying what it is waiting for. A board that cannot be reached
-- is waited out the same way — it is most often a board mid-restart, back
-- within the wait. And a renderer that cannot resolve its task — the wait
-- ran out, or the task has no worktree to root a tree at — stays open and
-- says so, rather than exiting. The one thing that closes a pane is still the
-- user's exit key (ExitAgentTreeRenderer, ExitAgentTreeDiffRenderer).
--
-- The tree and the diff pane follow the same three rules, separately: each is
-- its own process asking the board on its own, and neither waits on the
-- other.

rule AgentTreePaneResolvesItsTask {
    -- The task row became readable while the tree's renderer was waiting for
    -- it, and the task has a worktree: the pane proceeds as normal. The first
    -- refresh tick after this is the first git query (RefreshAgentTree).
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.task_arrived

    requires: board_task(pane.task).worktree != null

    ensures: pane.task_resolution = resolved

    @guidance
        -- A row already present when the renderer starts resolves at once —
        -- there is no minimum wait, and in the common case the waiting line
        -- is never seen.
        --
        -- The worktree and base branch come from this read and are not
        -- re-read afterwards. That is how the renderer behaved before the
        -- wait existed, and a task's worktree does not change under a live
        -- agent.
}

rule AgentTreePaneTaskHasNoWorktree {
    -- The row arrived, but the task has no worktree. There is no directory to
    -- root a tree at, and waiting longer will not give it one: give up now,
    -- without spending the rest of the wait.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.task_arrived

    requires: board_task(pane.task).worktree = null

    ensures: pane.task_resolution = unresolved
    ensures: pane.startup_failure = task_without_worktree_reason(pane.task)
    ensures: pane.status = shown
}

rule AgentTreePaneTaskNeverArrives {
    -- The wait ran out and the board never answered with the row. The pane
    -- says why — the task was not found, or the board could not be reached
    -- — and stays open saying it.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.renderer_started_at + config.agent_tree_startup_wait <= now

    requires: pane.task_resolution = waiting

    ensures: pane.task_resolution = unresolved
    ensures: pane.startup_failure = task_not_found_reason(pane.task)
    ensures: pane.status = shown

    @guidance
        -- pane.status = shown in all three failure outcomes (this rule, its
        -- no-worktree sibling and the diff pane's pair) is the guarantee, not
        -- a state change: the renderer keeps running and keeps the pane open.
        -- Exiting was the bug. A pane that closes itself leaves nothing to
        -- read; a pane that stays leaves the reason on screen until the user
        -- has read it and pressed the exit key.
        --
        -- There is no retry after giving up. The failure is on screen, and
        -- the toggle (ShowAgentTreePane, after a hide) starts a fresh
        -- renderer with a fresh wait for anyone who wants another go.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- board_task(task) is BoardPaneView's pane_view(task.id).task — null
        -- while the row has not arrived in the board's rows, and null while
        -- the board cannot be reached. The two reason functions name the task
        -- by id: task_without_worktree_reason reads "task <id> has no
        -- worktree"; task_not_found_reason reads "task <id> not found" when
        -- the board answered without the task, and, when the renderer's last
        -- attempt could not reach the board, says that instead, naming the
        -- address it tried — "not found" would send the user looking for a
        -- task that may well exist.
}

rule AgentTreeDiffPaneResolvesItsTask {
    -- The diff pane's counterpart of AgentTreePaneResolvesItsTask. Its first
    -- git read follows on its own timer (RefreshAgentTreeDiff).
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.diff_task_arrived

    requires: board_task(pane.task).worktree != null

    ensures: pane.diff_task_resolution = resolved
}

rule AgentTreeDiffPaneTaskHasNoWorktree {
    -- The diff pane's counterpart of AgentTreePaneTaskHasNoWorktree.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.diff_task_arrived

    requires: board_task(pane.task).worktree = null

    ensures: pane.diff_task_resolution = unresolved
    ensures: pane.diff_startup_failure = task_without_worktree_reason(pane.task)
}

rule AgentTreeDiffPaneTaskNeverArrives {
    -- The diff pane's counterpart of AgentTreePaneTaskNeverArrives, timed
    -- from the diff renderer's own start.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.diff_renderer_started_at + config.agent_tree_startup_wait <= now

    requires: pane.diff_pane != null
    requires: pane.diff_task_resolution = waiting

    ensures: pane.diff_task_resolution = unresolved
    ensures: pane.diff_startup_failure = task_not_found_reason(pane.task)

    @guidance
        -- The diff pane stays open saying why, exactly as the tree does. It
        -- is still closed by everything that closes it today — emptying the
        -- open set (CloseAgentTreeDiffPaneWhenEmpty), hiding its tree
        -- (KillAgentTreeDiffPaneWithItsTree), or the exit key inside it —
        -- because those are the user's choices, not the pane giving up.
        --
        -- pane.diff_pane != null guards the timer: diff_renderer_started_at
        -- is stamped at the pane's creation, long before any diff pane may
        -- exist, and no renderer is waiting until one does.
}

-- == Rendering ==

rule RefreshAgentTree {
    -- A shown pane redraws on a timer, asking git afresh each time what has
    -- changed in the worktree.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.last_rendered_at + config.agent_tree_refresh_interval <= now

    -- Hidden panes have no renderer process. The timestamp is not advanced
    -- while hidden, so the trigger stays satisfied and the first refresh
    -- happens immediately on show.
    requires: pane.status = shown

    -- The renderer knows its worktree and base branch. Until it does there is
    -- nothing to ask git about (see "Companion Pane Startup").
    requires: pane.task_resolution = resolved

    -- The git query succeeded. A failed query is the other rule
    -- (AgentTreeGitFailureKeepsLastGoodTree), and the two are mutually
    -- exclusive on this guard so exactly one of them fires per tick.
    requires: git_changes(pane.root, pane.task.base_branch).ok

    let changes = git_changes(pane.root, pane.task.base_branch).value

    ensures: pane.last_rendered_at = now
    ensures: pane.rendered_changes = changes

    -- A working git clears its own stale failure notice, and nothing else. A
    -- diff-open or agent-jump notice is the answer to a keypress the user made
    -- moments ago and survives every refresh until they press the next key; an
    -- agent-list notice belongs to the agents section's own timer, which
    -- clears it when its own read recovers.
    ensures: pane.error_notice =
        if old(pane.notice_source) = git_query: null
        else: old(pane.error_notice)

    ensures:
        for entry in changed_tree(changes):
            AgentTreeNodeRendered(
                pane: pane,
                node: AgentTreeNode {
                    pane_root: pane.root,
                    path: entry.path,
                    kind: entry.kind,
                    -- A file's badge is exactly what git said about it.
                    -- Directories are never badged: a directory is in the tree
                    -- only as a route to a changed file, and git says nothing
                    -- about directories.
                    badge:
                        if entry.kind = directory: null
                        else: change_for_path(changes, entry.path),
                    -- Auto-expand: a directory opens exactly when something
                    -- under it changed, so the path to every badge is visible
                    -- without the view degenerating into a full repo browser.
                    expanded: entry.kind = directory
                              and has_changed_descendant(changes, entry.path),
                    -- A file shows its own counts; a directory shows the sum
                    -- over the changed files beneath it. Both come from the
                    -- same query that produced the badges, so a row can never
                    -- show a count and a badge that disagree about what
                    -- happened.
                    counts:
                        if entry.kind = directory:
                            sum_counts_below(changes, entry.path)
                        else: counts_for_path(changes, entry.path),
                    -- Whether the diff pane is currently showing this file.
                    -- Read from the pane's open set, which the refresh never
                    -- writes: a tick redraws the marker, it does not decide it.
                    diff_open: entry.kind = file
                               and pane.open_diff_paths.contains(entry.path)
                }
            )

    @guidance
        -- == AgentTreeGitQuery: what "asking git" means ==
        -- Four steps, all run against pane.root and each bounded by
        -- config.agent_tree_git_timeout:
        --
        --   1. Resolve the baseline: this worktree's fork point from
        --      pane.task.base_branch, taking BOTH the local branch of that
        --      name and its remote-tracking counterpart into account. See
        --      AgentTreeBaselineIsTaskBaseBranch for why one of the two alone
        --      is not enough.
        --   2. A name-and-status diff of the WORKING TREE against that
        --      baseline, with rename detection off.
        --   3. A per-file added/removed line count of the same working-tree
        --      diff, against the same baseline.
        --   4. A listing of untracked files, honouring the repo's ignore
        --      rules.
        --
        -- Step 3 is a SEPARATE ask, not a richer form of step 2, and the two
        -- must agree on the baseline and on rename detection or a row's badge
        -- and its counts would describe different comparisons. It reports
        -- nothing for the untracked paths step 4 finds, which is where
        -- UntrackedFilesHaveNoLineCounts comes from — not from a decision this
        -- pane made, but from what a diff against the index can see. It also
        -- reports no counts for a binary file, and that is the same fact
        -- reaching the user by the same route.
        --
        -- A path present in step 2 but missing from step 3, or carrying a
        -- count that will not parse, is rendered with no counts rather than
        -- with a guess — the soft-fail-decoding convention again, and the same
        -- reasoning as CollapsedGitStatusLetters: one unreadable field must
        -- not cost the user the row.
        --
        -- Steps 2, 3 and 4 ask git for NUL-delimited output; step 1 emits
        -- commit ids, which need none. Git's default is to C-quote
        -- any path containing a non-ASCII byte, so a file whose name holds an
        -- accented letter comes back C-quoted, with the letter replaced by its
        -- octal escapes — which would be rendered as that literal string and
        -- would open nothing. NUL delimiting also makes a path containing a space, a tab
        -- or a newline unambiguous, and means no field may be trimmed: every
        -- byte between delimiters is part of the filename.
        --
        -- The second covers every tracked change the agent has made for this
        -- task, committed or not — that is the point of diffing the working
        -- tree against the fork point rather than against HEAD. An agent that
        -- commits mid-session does not watch its work vanish from the pane.
        -- The fourth covers files the agent created and has not staged, which
        -- a diff cannot see.
        --
        -- == CollapsedGitStatusLetters ==
        -- Git's status letters map onto FileChange as:
        --   A, and every untracked path from the second command -> added
        --   D                                                   -> deleted
        --   M, T (type change), and anything else recognised     -> modified
        -- A letter this mapping does not recognise is skipped rather than
        -- guessed at, in line with this repo's soft-fail-decoding convention,
        -- and so is a malformed output line. One unparseable line must not
        -- blank the tree.
        --
        -- Rename detection is OFF deliberately. With it on, a rename is one
        -- entry naming two paths, which the three-value FileChange vocabulary
        -- cannot express. With it off, git reports the same rename as a
        -- deletion of the old path and an addition of the new one — which is
        -- both true and exactly what the user wants to see in a file tree.
        --
        -- == ChangePrecedence: when both queries name one path ==
        -- The two overlap. `git rm --cached foo` leaves foo reported as a
        -- deletion by the diff AND listed as untracked, because it is out of
        -- the index but still on disk. The badge is resolved by precedence —
        -- added beats deleted beats modified — and NOT by which query ran
        -- first. Added wins that particular collision because the file is
        -- there, which is the more useful of the two true statements; deleted
        -- beats modified because a deletion is the more consequential fact and
        -- the one a file tree exists to show. Stating it as precedence rather
        -- than as "the later query wins" is what stops the order of two
        -- commands from silently deciding a badge.
        --
        -- == The four rendering rules, in one place ==
        --   1. ROOT. The tree is rooted at the pane's root — the task's
        --      worktree, never the parent repo. Node paths are relative to it.
        --      A node exists only for a changed path or an ancestor directory
        --      of one; an unchanged sibling never gets a node at all, so the
        --      tree is a projection of the git result, not of the filesystem.
        --      An ancestor directory whose only child is another directory
        --      does not get a node of its own: the chain is one node named by
        --      the whole route (NoSingleChildDirectoryChains).
        --   2. BADGES. Every file node carries exactly one of added, modified
        --      or deleted, taken from git. There is no precedence to resolve
        --      and no badge to retract: git reports one status per path, and
        --      the next refresh replaces it wholesale.
        --   3. EXPANSION. Every directory node is, by construction, an
        --      ancestor of a changed path, so every directory that appears is
        --      expanded — there is no unchanged, collapsed directory to
        --      reveal. A manual collapse is not overwritten by the next
        --      refresh; only newly-changed paths add new nodes.
        --
        --      BOTH halves hold across a refresh that merges or unmerges a
        --      chain and so RENAMES a row — see MergedDirectoryChainRows,
        --      which is the one way a directory's row can change identity
        --      between refreshes. They are mirror failures and an
        --      implementation that remembers the wrong thing gets exactly one
        --      of them: remember which rows were opened, keyed by row
        --      identity, and a renamed row springs back open after a manual
        --      collapse; key the same memory by path and a renamed row the
        --      user never touched comes back COLLAPSED, hiding a badge on a
        --      refresh nobody asked for. What satisfies both is remembering
        --      which PATHS the user closed, and opening everything else.
        --
        --      An explicit OPEN is never weaker than an explicit close: it
        --      clears the collapse on every link its row stands for, so a row
        --      the user has opened does not come back closed when a later
        --      refresh unmerges it and hands the collapse back to the link it
        --      was made on. A CLOSE records only the row it was made on, which
        --      is the asymmetry that makes an unmerge reveal an outer link
        --      that has since gained a changed file of its own while leaving
        --      the inner one shut.
        --
        --      A collapse is forgotten when its row goes — and "goes" means
        --      no row stands for that path any more, not merely that no row
        --      has it as its exact path. A merged row stands for every link it
        --      absorbed: collapse `a` while it holds a file of its own, let
        --      that file be reverted, and the row that replaces it is `a/b`,
        --      which must come up collapsed because it hides exactly what the
        --      collapse hid. Only when the path stops holding a change
        --      altogether is the collapse dropped — the agent reverts its last
        --      edit under `src/`, and a later edit there is news that opens
        --      the row, rather than carrying forward a collapse the user made
        --      about a different state of the worktree.
        --   4. ORDER. Siblings are ordered files first, then directories,
        --      each group by ONE name component — a directory's own name, or
        --      a merged row's FIRST segment, never its whole route. See the
        --      surface's RowsPutAFoldersOwnFilesFirst.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- git_changes(root, base) runs the four steps above and returns
        -- either the parsed set of GitFileChanges or a failure.
        -- changed_tree(changes) derives (path, kind) entries for every changed
        -- path and each of its ancestor directories, deduplicated, then merges
        -- every single-child directory chain into one entry whose path is the
        -- whole chain — see NoSingleChildDirectoryChains. Merging happens
        -- while the entries are being derived, before anything is rendered, so
        -- no node the invariant forbids ever reaches a row. An implementation
        -- that assembles the unmerged chain first and folds it afterwards
        -- satisfies this, and dispatch's does; what it may NOT do is merge
        -- after deciding order, expansion or counts, all three of which are
        -- properties of the merged shape.
        -- change_for_path / has_changed_descendant are pure lookups over the
        -- already-loaded change set, and so are counts_for_path and
        -- sum_counts_below. The sum skips descendants whose counts are null
        -- rather than reading them as zero, and is itself null when every
        -- descendant is null — see AgentTreeNode.counts for why "+0 -0" would
        -- be a lie there.
        --
        -- == Resolves TreeScanExclusions ==
        -- The predecessor of this rule left open which subtrees a fuller
        -- worktree traversal should skip, because an unfiltered eager scan of
        -- a large repo is slow and mostly noise. The question is now moot and
        -- the open question is removed: git already answers exactly the
        -- question the scan was meant to approximate, and it already applies
        -- the repo's own ignore rules. There is no traversal to exclude
        -- anything from.
        --
        -- The consequence is unchanged and intended: an unchanged worktree
        -- renders an EMPTY tree, not a repo browser. The pane answers "what
        -- has changed here", not "what is here".
        --
        -- == Only rebuild when the answer changes ==
        -- The tick runs the query every time, but re-syncing expansion is
        -- skipped when the derived tree is identical to the one already
        -- rendered. The comparison is on the TREE, not on the raw change set:
        -- the tree is what the user sees, and two change sets differing only
        -- by a duplicate entry render the same. This is a performance
        -- short-circuit with one behavioural consequence worth stating: the
        -- user's manual expansion state is preserved across ticks precisely
        -- because nothing is re-synced on a no-change tick.
        --
        -- == Auto-expansion must be verified by RENDERING ==
        -- The EXPANSION clause above has to be covered by rendering a deeply
        -- nested changed path and asserting the LEAF is on screen — not by
        -- inspecting the tree widget's open-set. Asking the widget to open a
        -- directory reports success even when the key identifies no node, so
        -- an open-set assertion can encode the wrong key and pass while every
        -- nested directory renders collapsed. That was the shipped behaviour
        -- until #3811, under a green open-set test.
}

rule AgentTreeGitFailureKeepsLastGoodTree {
    -- The same timer tick, when git could not answer: NEITHER ref of the base
    -- branch exists (one of the two is enough — see
    -- AgentTreeBaselineIsTaskBaseBranch), the index is locked, the worktree is
    -- gone, git is not on PATH, or the query overran
    -- config.agent_tree_git_timeout.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.last_rendered_at + config.agent_tree_refresh_interval <= now

    requires: pane.status = shown
    requires: pane.task_resolution = resolved
    requires: not git_changes(pane.root, pane.task.base_branch).ok

    -- Stamped anyway, so the next tick is a normal tick rather than an
    -- immediate retry: a persistently broken git must not spin.
    ensures: pane.last_rendered_at = now

    -- The last good tree stays on screen. Not restated as an assignment
    -- because the point is the ABSENCE of one — rendered_changes is the only
    -- input to the tree, and this rule does not touch it.
    ensures: pane.rendered_changes = old(pane.rendered_changes)

    ensures: pane.error_notice = git_failure_reason(pane.root, pane.task.base_branch)
    ensures: pane.notice_source = git_query
    ensures: pane.status = shown

    @guidance
        -- == Why keep the tree rather than blank it ==
        -- The commonest failure by far is a transient index lock, taken by the
        -- agent's own git commands a few times a minute. Blanking on that
        -- would make the pane flicker empty during exactly the moments the
        -- user most wants to watch it. A stale tree that says it is stale is
        -- more useful than an empty one that cannot say why.
        --
        -- The notice and the reddened border are what make this honest — see
        -- AgentTreeNoticeRedensBorder. Without them, keeping the tree would be
        -- indistinguishable from the tree being correct.
        --
        -- == Same notice channel as a failed diff-pane split ==
        -- error_notice is one field with two writers, and it is cleared by the
        -- next keypress (ClearAgentTreeErrorNotice) regardless of which one set
        -- it. Clearing a git-failure notice on a keypress is not a lie: if git
        -- is still failing, the very next tick sets it again. The user is never
        -- left looking at a stale tree with no notice for longer than one
        -- refresh interval.
        --
        -- == Not the same as an empty result ==
        -- A successful query returning nothing is RefreshAgentTree with an
        -- empty change set: the tree legitimately empties, and no notice is
        -- shown. Only a query that could not run reaches this rule. The two
        -- must never be conflated — "you have changed nothing" and "I could not
        -- find out" are different statements.
}

-- == Companion Pane Input ==
--
-- Keys pressed inside the companion pane are handled by the renderer process
-- itself, with no tmux prefix and no involvement from the board TUI. Only the
-- exit key has a consequence outside the renderer's own view, so only it gets a
-- rule; the cursor and expansion keys are specified on the
-- AgentTreeCompanionPane surface (see the AgentTreeViewState open question).

rule ExitAgentTreeRenderer {
    -- q, or Ctrl-C, stops the renderer. tmux closes a pane when the command
    -- running in it exits, so the companion pane goes with it. The pane is
    -- bound by the trigger rather than looked up: the keystroke arrived inside
    -- one specific renderer, which knows which pane it is running in.
    when: ExitAgentTreeRenderer(user, pane)

    requires: pane.status = shown

    ensures: pane.status = hidden
    ensures: pane.tmux_pane = null

    @guidance
        -- Reaches the same hidden state HideAgentTreePane describes, by a
        -- different route: the renderer returns from its event loop rather than
        -- the board issuing kill-pane. Nothing is lost either way, since the
        -- whole tree is re-derived from a fresh git query.
        --
        -- Nothing is missed while no pane is open. The agent keeps changing
        -- files, and a renderer started later sees all of it in one query,
        -- because git is asked about the worktree's current state rather than
        -- replayed from a record kept while nobody was watching. Pressing the
        -- toggle key brings the tree straight back, fully populated
        -- (ShowAgentTreePane).
}

rule OpenAgentTreeFileDiff {
    -- Space or Enter on a file node whose diff is not open: open it.
    when: ToggleSelectedAgentTreeFileDiff(user, pane, node)

    requires: pane.focus = tree
    requires: node.kind = file
    requires: not pane.open_diff_paths.contains(node.path)

    ensures: pane.open_diff_paths = old(pane.open_diff_paths).append(node.path)
    ensures: AgentTreeDiffOpenSetChanged(pane: pane)

    @guidance
        -- == A deleted file opens like any other ==
        -- This is the one place the behaviour reverses from what it replaced.
        -- The editor this used to launch was REFUSED on a node badged deleted,
        -- because there was no file to hand it and most editors would have
        -- shown a misleading empty buffer. A diff has the opposite property:
        -- the deleted file's content is exactly what git prints for it, so the
        -- deleted case is the one where opening it is most useful.
        --
        -- An open question used to sit at the foot of this spec asking
        -- precisely this — whether the pane should show content git holds but
        -- the worktree does not, and through which key. It is answered and
        -- removed: through this key, like every other file. There is no
        -- deleted-file guard anywhere in this rule set, and no refusal rule
        -- paired with it.
        --
        -- == Nothing is read from disk ==
        -- The pane does not open the file, stat it, or check it exists. The
        -- diff comes from git, which answers about the baseline and the index
        -- as well as the working tree, so a path that is gone from disk still
        -- has a diff. The file_exists check the editor rules needed has no
        -- counterpart here and is not an omission.
}

rule CloseAgentTreeFileDiff {
    -- Space or Enter on a file node whose diff IS open: close it. The same key
    -- both ways, which is what makes it a toggle rather than an open command
    -- with a separate close to remember.
    when: ToggleSelectedAgentTreeFileDiff(user, pane, node)

    requires: pane.focus = tree
    requires: node.kind = file
    requires: pane.open_diff_paths.contains(node.path)

    ensures: pane.open_diff_paths = old(pane.open_diff_paths).remove(node.path)
    ensures: AgentTreeDiffOpenSetChanged(pane: pane)
}

rule OpenAllAgentTreeFileDiffs {
    -- The all-files key, pressed while nothing is open: open every changed
    -- file's diff at once.
    when: ToggleAllAgentTreeFileDiffs(user, pane)

    requires: pane.open_diff_paths.is_empty()

    ensures: pane.open_diff_paths = changed_file_paths(pane.rendered_changes)
    ensures: AgentTreeDiffOpenSetChanged(pane: pane)

    @guidance
        -- Reads from rendered_changes, the last SUCCESSFUL query, and not from
        -- a fresh one. Two consequences, both wanted: the key acts on exactly
        -- the tree the user is looking at, and it still works while git is
        -- failing and the pane is showing its last good tree.
        --
        -- changed_file_paths is the paths of the tree's FILE nodes, not the raw
        -- paths of the change set, and the difference is load-bearing in two
        -- ways. Directories are excluded, because a directory has no contents
        -- of its own to diff (OnlyFilesOpenDiffs). And a path the tree rejected
        -- for escaping the root — which changed_tree drops and git never
        -- produces — has no row, so it must not be openable either; taking the
        -- change set directly would put a path in the open set that the user
        -- can neither see nor close.
        --
        -- Every changed file, with no cap on how many. The per-file byte cap
        -- (config.agent_tree_diff_max_bytes) is what bounds the cost, and it
        -- bounds it per file whether one file was opened or four hundred were.
        -- A separate "too many files" refusal here would only move the same
        -- limit somewhere the user cannot see it coming.
}

rule CloseAllAgentTreeFileDiffs {
    -- The same key, pressed while ANYTHING is open: close everything. Not
    -- "close what I opened with this key" — the two directions are decided by
    -- whether the set is empty, so one press always empties a non-empty set
    -- however it got filled.
    when: ToggleAllAgentTreeFileDiffs(user, pane)

    requires: not pane.open_diff_paths.is_empty()

    ensures: pane.open_diff_paths = []
    ensures: AgentTreeDiffOpenSetChanged(pane: pane)
}

rule SplitAgentTreeDiffPane {
    -- The open set changed to something non-empty and there is no pane to show
    -- it in: split one beneath the tree.
    when: AgentTreeDiffOpenSetChanged(pane)

    requires: not pane.open_diff_paths.is_empty()
    requires: pane.diff_pane = null

    ensures: pane.diff_pane = core/TmuxPane.created()
    ensures: pane.error_notice = null

    -- The diff pane's renderer is its own process and resolves the task for
    -- itself (AgentTreeDiffPaneResolvesItsTask and its two siblings).
    ensures: pane.diff_task_resolution = waiting
    ensures: pane.diff_renderer_started_at = now
    ensures: pane.diff_startup_failure = null

    @guidance
        -- == Geometry ==
        -- The pane subdivides the COMPANION PANE'S column, taking
        -- config.agent_tree_diff_pane_percent of its height beneath the tree.
        -- It deliberately does NOT span the window: the agent's own pane keeps
        -- every column it had, so opening a diff never takes room from the
        -- thing the user is supervising. This is the one geometric difference
        -- from the editor pane this replaced, and it is the reason the split
        -- targets the tree pane rather than the window.
        --
        -- == Focus does not move ==
        -- The keystroke that opens a diff leaves tmux focus in the tree, so
        -- browsing stays a one-handed loop: move the cursor, open, move on.
        -- Reaching the diff to scroll it is a deliberate second act — the tmux
        -- prefix plus a direction, tmux's own pane navigation and nothing this
        -- spec invents. That is also why the diff pane needs no focus
        -- indicator of its own: tmux already draws one.
        --
        -- == Why this is a rule and not an ensures on the four toggles ==
        -- A user can kill the diff pane themselves, with tmux or by pressing
        -- the exit key inside it, while the open set is still non-empty
        -- (ExitAgentTreeDiffRenderer). Routing every set change through this
        -- one rule is what makes the next toggle bring the pane back rather
        -- than leaving the user with a set of open files and nowhere to see
        -- them. The repair is deliberately NOT on the refresh tick: a pane the
        -- user closed a second ago must stay closed until they ask again.
        --
        -- One consequence is accepted and slightly odd: pressing Space to
        -- CLOSE one file, while the pane is gone and other files remain open,
        -- re-splits it. The alternative is to distinguish opening presses from
        -- closing ones here, which buys consistency in a case the user reaches
        -- only after killing the pane by hand.
}

rule CloseAgentTreeDiffPaneWhenEmpty {
    -- The last open file was closed: the pane has nothing to show, so it goes.
    when: AgentTreeDiffOpenSetChanged(pane)

    requires: pane.open_diff_paths.is_empty()
    requires: pane.diff_pane != null

    ensures: pane.diff_pane = null

    @guidance
        -- The pane's presence IS the answer to "is anything open", which is
        -- what DiffPaneExistsWhenSomethingIsOpen states. Leaving an empty pane
        -- behind would make the user close it a second time, by a second
        -- mechanism, to get their rows back.
}

rule KillAgentTreeDiffPaneWithItsTree {
    -- The tree pane went away — the global toggle hid it, or its renderer
    -- exited. The diff pane goes with it, and the open set goes with them.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.status transitions_to hidden

    ensures: pane.diff_pane = null
    ensures: pane.open_diff_paths = []

    @guidance
        -- A diff pane outliving its tree is orphaned: nothing drives its open
        -- set, nothing refreshes it, and the toggle that would bring the tree
        -- back does not act on it. So hiding takes both.
        --
        -- The open set is cleared rather than kept, which makes showing the
        -- tree again give a bare tree and no diff pane. That is the same
        -- treatment the cursor position and the manual expansion state already
        -- get (see the pane surface's guidance): view state does not survive a
        -- restart of the renderer, and the open set is view state. Nothing is
        -- lost that a keypress does not bring straight back, because the
        -- diffs were never stored — they are re-derived from git.
}

rule AgentTreeDiffPaneFailureIsVisible {
    -- Opening the diff pane can fail for reasons the user can neither see nor
    -- predict from the tree: tmux refused the split, or the renderer cannot
    -- tell which pane it is running in. Say so in the tree's border.
    when: AgentTreeDiffPaneOpenFailed(pane, reason)

    ensures: pane.error_notice = reason
    ensures: pane.notice_source = diff_open
    ensures: pane.status = shown

    @guidance
        -- A silent no-op is indistinguishable from a broken key, and the user
        -- would press it again. This is the direct response to a keypress, so
        -- it must say something.
        --
        -- The notice names the reason and nothing else — there is one line of
        -- border to say it in. It is also logged, because the pane's copy is
        -- gone by the next keypress (see ClearAgentTreeErrorNotice).
        --
        -- The renderer keeps running: a diff pane that cannot be opened must
        -- not take the tree with it. pane.status = shown above is that
        -- guarantee, not a state change.
        --
        -- Two failures, not the four its predecessor covered. The deleted-file
        -- refusal and the file-is-gone case are gone with the editor, because a
        -- diff needs no file on disk; the out-of-root case is gone because no
        -- path is handed to anything that resolves it — the diff pane is given
        -- the task, and re-derives every path from git itself.
        --
        -- The open set is left ALONE by a failed split. The user asked for a
        -- file to be open and it is open; what failed is showing it. The next
        -- toggle retries the split (SplitAgentTreeDiffPane) rather than
        -- requiring the user to re-open everything.
}

rule RefreshAgentTreeDiff {
    -- The diff pane redraws on the same timer the tree does, asking git afresh
    -- for the contents of whatever is currently open.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.last_diff_rendered_at + config.agent_tree_refresh_interval <= now

    requires: pane.diff_pane != null
    requires: pane.diff_task_resolution = resolved

    ensures: pane.last_diff_rendered_at = now

    ensures:
        for path in pane.open_diff_paths:
            AgentTreeFileDiffRendered(
                pane: pane,
                diff: FileDiff {
                    path: path,
                    -- Exactly one of these is non-null. Git either printed the
                    -- change or said why it would not, and the pane shows
                    -- whichever it got (see DiffBodyExcludesRefusal).
                    body:    git_file_diff(pane.root, pane.task.base_branch, path).body,
                    refusal: git_file_diff(pane.root, pane.task.base_branch, path).refusal
                }
            )

    @guidance
        -- == The same baseline, asked the same way ==
        -- file_diff resolves the baseline exactly as AgentTreeGitQuery's step 1
        -- does, and diffs the working tree against it exactly as step 2 does.
        -- It has to: a diff taken against a different baseline than the badge
        -- beside it would show the user two answers to one question. The
        -- baseline is re-derived here rather than passed across from the tree,
        -- because the two panes are separate processes and a value handed
        -- between them is a value that can go stale.
        --
        -- == Order ==
        -- Diffs are rendered in the order the paths appear in the TREE, not in
        -- the order the user opened them. The tree is the index the user reads
        -- the pane through, so the two must scroll the same way.
        --
        -- Tree order is NOT the paths' own lexicographic order, and stopped
        -- being it when a folder's own files started sorting ahead of its
        -- subfolders (RowsPutAFoldersOwnFilesFirst): a top-level `z.rs` has a
        -- row above `src/lib.rs` while sorting after it as a path. This pane
        -- holds paths and never sees the tree, so it must apply that rule to
        -- the paths itself rather than take a sorted set of them as already
        -- being in row order. An implementation with a second expression of
        -- the ordering rule — one over nodes, one over paths — owes a test
        -- that the two agree; dispatch has both and pins them together.
        --
        -- == Refusals ==
        -- A file is rendered as a placeholder rather than as contents in
        -- exactly three cases, and each is a DiffRefusal value carried on the
        -- FileDiff rather than a notice on the tree: the path is untracked, so
        -- git has nothing to diff; git reports it binary; or its diff exceeds
        -- config.agent_tree_diff_max_bytes. A refusal is a fact about that one
        -- file and must not cost the user the other diffs in the pane, which is
        -- why it is not routed through error_notice.
        --
        -- == A path with nothing to show is not an error ==
        -- The open set holds paths, and a path can stop being reported by git
        -- between the press that opened it and this tick — the agent reverted
        -- the file, or committed and reset. Such a path contributes no entry
        -- and no placeholder. It is not pruned from the open set either: the
        -- agent may change the file again, and the user did not ask for it to
        -- be closed. See OpenDiffPathsMaySurviveTheirFiles.
        --
        -- == Only re-read the diffs when the answer changes ==
        -- Fetching every open file's contents is the expensive part, and the
        -- tick would otherwise pay it once a second forever. So the pass first
        -- asks for the open paths' line counts — one command git answers from
        -- the index without materialising a patch — and re-reads the contents
        -- only when that answer, or the open set itself, has moved since the
        -- last pass. The steady-state cost of the poll is therefore two short
        -- git processes however many files are open.
        --
        -- The same short-circuit RefreshAgentTree applies to its own query, and
        -- it has the same one behavioural consequence worth stating: the user's
        -- scroll position survives a tick precisely because nothing is rebuilt
        -- on a no-change tick.
        --
        -- An untracked path never appears in that answer and does not need to:
        -- its placeholder is the same whatever the file says. A path that
        -- BECOMES tracked does change it, which is the transition that has to
        -- be caught.
        --
        -- == Failure is the tree's story, not the pane's ==
        -- A git failure here leaves the last good diffs on screen, the same way
        -- AgentTreeGitFailureKeepsLastGoodTree leaves the last good tree. The
        -- two panes fail independently because they run independently.
        --
        -- == Black box ==
        -- git_file_diff(root, base, path) resolves the baseline, takes the
        -- working-tree diff of that one path against it, and returns either the
        -- body or the DiffRefusal that stopped it — never both and never
        -- neither.
}

rule ClearAgentTreeErrorNotice {
    -- Any key the renderer handles acknowledges a notice. There is no separate
    -- dismiss key and no timer.
    when: AgentTreeKeyPressed(pane)

    requires: pane.error_notice != null

    ensures: pane.error_notice = null
    ensures: pane.notice_source = null

    @guidance
        -- The next keypress is the earliest moment the user has demonstrably
        -- seen the notice, which makes it the right thing to clear on: a timer
        -- could expire before they looked, and a dedicated dismiss key would be
        -- one more binding to document for a one-line message.
        --
        -- AgentTreeKeyPressed covers every key on the AgentTreeCompanionPane
        -- surface, the exit key included — clearing state on the way out is
        -- harmless, and the alternative is enumerating exceptions.
}

-- == Agents Section ==
--
-- Beneath the tree, the pane lists every task that has a live agent window, so
-- the user can see which agents are running without going back to the board,
-- and jump straight to one. Its main use is finding the task id to hand an
-- agent that should message another one; the jump is what makes the list
-- something to act on rather than only read.

rule RefreshAgentTreeAgentList {
    -- A shown pane re-reads the board's task list on its own timer.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.last_agents_listed_at + config.agent_tree_agents_refresh_interval <= now

    requires: pane.status = shown
    -- The agents section starts with the rest of the pane, once the task is
    -- resolved; a pane that never resolves never lists agents.
    requires: pane.task_resolution = resolved
    requires: board_tasks().ok

    let live = board_tasks().value.filter(t => is_live_agent(t))

    ensures: pane.last_agents_listed_at = now
    ensures: pane.listed_agents = sorted_by_id(live)

    -- A working read clears its own stale notice and nothing else, exactly
    -- as RefreshAgentTree does for git.
    ensures: pane.error_notice =
        if old(pane.notice_source) = agent_list: null
        else: old(pane.error_notice)

    @guidance
        -- == What "live" means ==
        -- is_live_agent(t) holds when t is in the Running or Review column and
        -- the board has a tmux window recorded for it. That is the board's
        -- own answer to "does this task have an agent window", read as it
        -- stands; the pane does not ask tmux itself. A window the board has
        -- not yet noticed is gone is listed until it does, and a jump to it
        -- fails visibly (AgentTreeAgentJumpFailureIsVisible) rather than
        -- silently.
        --
        -- Every repo and every epic is listed, not only this task's. The
        -- question the section answers is "which agents can I reach", and an
        -- agent in another repo is as reachable as one in this.
        --
        -- == Read-only ==
        -- board_tasks() is BoardPaneView's pane_view(pane.task.id).live_agents
        -- — the board's own answer, already filtered by the same definition
        -- (LiveIsTheBoardsOwnDefinition), so the filter above restates it
        -- rather than narrowing it. Not ok when the board could not be
        -- reached or its answer could not be read. The pane never writes to
        -- the board (AskingChangesNothing) and never opens the store
        -- (PanesReadThroughTheBoard).
        --
        -- == Order ==
        -- sorted_by_id orders by task id, ascending. The order therefore never
        -- changes under the cursor except when an agent starts or finishes.
        --
        -- == The cursor follows its task ==
        -- A read that changes the list keeps the agents cursor on the task it
        -- was on, not on the row index: an agent starting above it must not
        -- move the selection onto a different agent. When the selected task
        -- has left the list, the cursor clamps to the last row.
        --
        -- == Latency ==
        -- The read runs beside the renderer, not inside its key loop, so a
        -- slow board never delays a keypress. The price is that a new list
        -- can take up to about twice the interval to reach the screen. That
        -- is fine for a list that changes on the scale of minutes.
}

rule AgentTreeAgentListFailureKeepsLastList {
    -- The same tick, when the board could not be reached or its answer
    -- could not be read.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.last_agents_listed_at + config.agent_tree_agents_refresh_interval <= now

    requires: pane.status = shown
    requires: pane.task_resolution = resolved
    requires: not board_tasks().ok

    -- Stamped anyway, so an absent board does not spin.
    ensures: pane.last_agents_listed_at = now
    ensures: pane.listed_agents = old(pane.listed_agents)
    ensures: pane.error_notice = board_tasks_failure_reason()
    ensures: pane.notice_source = agent_list
    ensures: pane.status = shown

    @guidance
        -- The same reasoning as AgentTreeGitFailureKeepsLastGoodTree: a stale
        -- list that says it is stale beats an empty one that cannot say why,
        -- and "no agents are running" and "I could not find out" must never
        -- read the same.
}

rule SwitchAgentTreeFocus {
    -- Tab moves the pane's keys between the tree and the agents section.
    when: SwitchAgentTreeFocus(user, pane)

    requires: pane.status = shown

    ensures: pane.focus =
        if old(pane.focus) = tree: agents
        else: tree

    @guidance
        -- A toggle, not a cycle, because there are exactly two sections. Tab
        -- works whether or not the agents section has any rows: an empty
        -- section is a real place to be, and refusing the key would read as a
        -- broken key.
}

rule JumpToAgentWindow {
    -- Space or Enter on an agent row switches tmux to that agent's window.
    when: JumpToAgentWindow(user, pane, target)

    requires: pane.focus = agents
    requires: pane.listed_agents.contains(target)

    -- The pane's own task is already on screen: jumping to it is a no-op,
    -- not an error, so it neither switches nor raises a notice.
    requires: target != pane.task

    ensures: AgentWindowSelected(window: target.tmux_window)

    @guidance
        -- The jump selects the window only. It does not move focus into the
        -- target's own agent-tree pane, and it changes nothing in this pane:
        -- when the user comes back, the cursor is where they left it.
        --
        -- The window is named by the task, not by the text on the row, so a
        -- title that changed since the last read still jumps to the right
        -- agent.
}

rule AgentTreeAgentJumpFailureIsVisible {
    -- The selected agent's window could not be selected — most often because
    -- it closed since the list was last read.
    when: AgentTreeAgentJumpFailed(pane, reason)

    ensures: pane.error_notice = reason
    ensures: pane.notice_source = agent_jump
    ensures: pane.status = shown

    @guidance
        -- The direct answer to a keypress, so it must say something, for the
        -- same reason AgentTreeDiffPaneFailureIsVisible does. The next list
        -- read drops a closed window's row on its own; this notice only has
        -- to cover the second in between.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- The node-scoped black boxes below (change_for_node,
-- has_changed_descendant_node, is_tracked_node, sum_counts_below_node,
-- has_lone_directory_child_node) are the
-- node-only counterparts of RefreshAgentTree's change-set-scoped ones
-- (change_for_path, has_changed_descendant, counts_for_path,
-- sum_counts_below): an invariant is handed a rendered node, not the
-- change set the refresh pass had in scope, so it re-derives the change for
-- that node from its pane_root and path.
--
-- has_lone_directory_child_node(n) is the exception: it asks about the
-- rendered tree's own shape rather than about the change set, and holds when n
-- has exactly one child node and that child is a directory.

-- One companion pane per agent window. A second split in the same window would
-- steal room from the agent's own pane and leave two renderers querying git
-- against the same worktree.
invariant OneCompanionPanePerAgentWindow {
    for a in AgentTreePanes:
        for b in AgentTreePanes:
            a != b implies a.window != b.window
}

-- At most one diff pane per agent window, and never shared between windows.
-- A second would divide the tree's column again on every open and leave the
-- older one unowned: nothing refreshes it, nothing drives its open set, and
-- only the tree that opened it knows it is there.
--
-- Held by construction rather than enforced: SplitAgentTreeDiffPane only fires
-- when pane.diff_pane is null, and the pane it creates is marked immediately
-- after the split with the diff value of the same role marker the toggle reads
-- (see HideAgentTreePane's "How the companion pane is identified"). One gap
-- follows from that and is accepted, exactly as it was for the editor pane it
-- replaces: if the mark cannot be written, the pane is already open and
-- showing the diffs, so the split reports success and a later split, finding
-- no mark, could make a second. Failing the open instead would report a
-- failure that did not happen, over a bookkeeping write.
invariant OneDiffPanePerAgentWindow {
    for a in AgentTreePanes:
        for b in AgentTreePanes:
            a != b implies (a.diff_pane = null or a.diff_pane != b.diff_pane)
}

-- The diff pane and a non-empty open set exist together or not at all. Both
-- directions matter and each closes a different hole: a pane with nothing open
-- is an empty frame the user has to dismiss by a second mechanism, and an open
-- set with no pane is a set of files marked open in the tree with nowhere to
-- read them.
--
-- The two rules that maintain it are complements on one trigger:
-- SplitAgentTreeDiffPane covers non-empty-with-no-pane and
-- CloseAgentTreeDiffPaneWhenEmpty covers empty-with-a-pane, so every change to
-- the open set restores this within the same keypress.
--
-- The user killing the pane by hand breaks it, deliberately and briefly: it
-- holds again at the next diff toggle, which is the behaviour
-- SplitAgentTreeDiffPane's guidance argues for.
invariant DiffPaneExistsWhenSomethingIsOpen {
    for p in AgentTreePanes:
        (p.diff_pane = null) = p.open_diff_paths.is_empty()
}

-- Only files ever have an open diff. A directory has no contents of its own —
-- it is in the tree solely as a route to changed files — so Space and Enter on
-- one toggle its expansion instead, and nothing can put its path in the open
-- set.
invariant OnlyFilesOpenDiffs {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        n.diff_open implies n.kind = file
}

-- A row's open marker says exactly what the diff pane is showing. The tree is
-- the index the user navigates the diff pane through, so a marker that
-- disagreed with the pane would send them looking for a diff that is not
-- there, or past one that is.
invariant NodeDiffOpenMatchesOpenSet {
    for p in AgentTreePanes:
        for n in AgentTreeNodes:
            (n.pane_root = p.root and n.kind = file)
                implies n.diff_open = p.open_diff_paths.contains(n.path)
}

-- An untracked file never carries line counts, and it is the only kind of
-- file that never can. This is not a display choice: a diff against the
-- baseline cannot see a path that is not in the index, so there is no number
-- to show and the pane declines to invent one. Showing "+0 -0" for a file the
-- agent just wrote would be the single most misleading row the pane could
-- draw.
--
-- The same fact reaches the diff pane as DiffRefusal.untracked, which is why
-- the placeholder there repeats git's own advice to stage the file rather than
-- reporting an error.
invariant UntrackedFilesHaveNoLineCounts {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        (n.kind = file and change_for_node(n) = added and not is_tracked_node(n))
            implies n.counts = null
}

-- A directory's counts are the sum of the changed files beneath it, never
-- anything else, and null only when nothing beneath it has counts at all.
-- Skipping the null descendants rather than reading them as zero is what keeps
-- a directory holding one modified file and three brand-new ones from
-- under-reporting itself as the modified file alone plus a false zero.
invariant DirectoryCountsSumDescendants {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        n.kind = directory implies n.counts = sum_counts_below_node(n)
}

-- No directory node holds a single child that is itself a directory. Such a
-- pair carries nothing the merged row does not: the outer directory holds only
-- the route to the inner one, so two rows, two indents and two identical count
-- sums say what one row says. A repo laid out several levels deep would
-- otherwise spend most of a narrow pane drawing the route to its files rather
-- than the files.
--
-- Stated as a property of the rendered tree rather than as a merging step, so
-- it holds however the tree was assembled and for a chain of any length. A
-- directory holding a changed file of its own is never merged away by it,
-- because such a directory either has more than one child or its one child is
-- a file — which is what keeps a file's own parent visible as the row above
-- it. See the surface's MergedDirectoryChainRows for what the merged row
-- looks like.
--
-- The pane root is outside this, because it is not an AgentTreeNode at all —
-- see the value's own doc. A tree whose only top-level row is a directory is
-- therefore fine and ordinary.
invariant NoSingleChildDirectoryChains {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        n.kind = directory implies not has_lone_directory_child_node(n)
}

-- A rendered diff carries contents or a reason, never both and never neither.
-- The pane always has something to draw for an open file, and the user is
-- never left looking at a blank region wondering whether it is loading.
invariant DiffBodyExcludesRefusal {
    for d in FileDiffs:
        (d.body = null) = (d.refusal != null)
}

-- A pane is always rooted at its task's worktree — never at the parent repo,
-- and never at another task's worktree. This is what the real-tmux harness
-- observes directly: a pane whose resolved cwd is the parent repo is the #231
-- failure mode the split hook exists to prevent.
invariant TaskPaneRootIsTaskWorktree {
    for p in AgentTreePanes:
        p.root = p.task.worktree?.path
}

-- A file node's badge is exactly what git said about that path, never anything
-- the renderer inferred. This is the whole design in one line: there is no
-- second source to disagree with, no precedence to resolve, and no way for a
-- badge to outlive the fact behind it.
invariant BadgeIsWhatGitSaid {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        n.kind = file implies n.badge = change_for_node(n)
}

-- Every file node has a badge. The contrapositive is the fix for the bug this
-- design exists to close: a path git does not report has no node at all, so
-- there is no unbadged file node sitting in the tree implying "touched, but
-- unchanged".
invariant EveryFileNodeIsBadged {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        n.kind = file implies n.badge != null
}

-- Expansion is a directory-only property, and it follows changed descendants
-- exactly: no expanded file nodes, no expanded-but-unchanged directories, no
-- collapsed directory hiding a badge on first render.
invariant DirectoryExpansionFollowsChangedDescendants {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        (n.expanded implies n.kind = directory)
        and (n.kind = directory implies n.expanded = has_changed_descendant_node(n))
}

-- Files are never badged as directories are: a directory carries no badge, so a
-- badge on a node always means "git reports this exact file as changed".
invariant OnlyFilesCarryBadges {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        n.kind = directory implies n.badge = null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface AgentTreeCompanionPane {
    facing user: User

    -- One surface instance per companion pane. The user reading it is looking
    -- at the agent's own tmux window, not at the kanban board.
    context pane: AgentTreePane

    exposes:
        pane.status
        pane.root
        pane.task
        pane.open_diff_paths
        pane.error_notice
        pane.focus
        pane.listed_agents
        pane.task_resolution
        pane.startup_failure when pane.task_resolution = unresolved

    contracts:
        demands BoardPaneView

    provides:
        -- The global tmux binding (prefix + config.agent_tree_toggle_key).
        -- Acts on the pane belonging to the currently focused window. See
        -- HideAgentTreePane / ShowAgentTreePane / BindAgentTreeToggle for the
        -- implementation.
        ToggleAgentTreePane(user)

        -- Keys handled by the renderer inside the pane itself, unprefixed,
        -- while the pane has tmux focus. Every one but the exit key acts on
        -- the view only. Each one-step cursor and expansion action carries a
        -- vim motion and an arrow key bound to the same action; the two are
        -- exact aliases, so nothing distinguishes which one the user pressed.
        -- Toggle is the exception, keyed by Space and Enter, and so are the
        -- four jump motions below, which are vim-only: Home/End/PageUp/PageDown
        -- are deliberately left unbound.
        ExitAgentTreeRenderer(user, pane)              -- q, Ctrl-C
        MoveAgentTreeCursorUp(user, pane)              -- k, Up
        MoveAgentTreeCursorDown(user, pane)            -- j, Down

        -- The four jump motions all move the cursor within the pane's list of
        -- *visible* rows — the rows a collapsed directory hides are skipped, so
        -- a jump never lands the cursor on something the user cannot see, and
        -- never opens or closes a directory to get there. All four clamp: a
        -- jump that would run past either end stops at that end, and on an
        -- empty tree every one of them is a no-op.
        JumpAgentTreeCursorToTop(user, pane)           -- gg
        JumpAgentTreeCursorToBottom(user, pane)        -- G

        -- Half a page is half the pane's current visible height, so the
        -- distance tracks how tall the pane has been dragged. A pane too short
        -- to show two rows still moves the cursor by one, never zero.
        MoveAgentTreeCursorHalfPageDown(user, pane)    -- Ctrl-D
        MoveAgentTreeCursorHalfPageUp(user, pane)      -- Ctrl-U

        -- Collapse doubles as "step out": when the selected node is already
        -- closed (or is a file), the cursor moves to its parent instead.
        CollapseSelectedAgentTreeNode(user, pane)      -- h, Left

        -- Expand acts on directories only. On a file node it is a no-op: a
        -- directory is the only thing with something to open, so the pane's
        -- expansion state is left untouched and a following collapse steps out
        -- to the parent on the first press rather than the second.
        ExpandSelectedAgentTreeNode(user, pane)        -- l, Right

        -- Space and Enter dispatch on the selected node's kind, so the two
        -- actions below never contend for them: a directory toggles its
        -- expansion, a file toggles its diff. Node expansion is therefore
        -- directory-only in the same way expand is, and the diff toggle is
        -- file-only in the same way (OnlyFilesOpenDiffs).
        ToggleSelectedAgentTreeNode(user, pane)           -- Space, Enter (directory)
        ToggleSelectedAgentTreeFileDiff(user, pane, node) -- Space, Enter (file, tree focus)

        -- The bulk form of the file toggle, on its own key. It does not
        -- dispatch on the selection at all: it acts on the whole tree, whatever
        -- the cursor happens to be on, a directory or nothing included.
        -- Direction is decided by the set rather than by a remembered mode — a
        -- non-empty set empties, an empty one fills — so one key always has one
        -- meaning for the state the user can see.
        ToggleAllAgentTreeFileDiffs(user, pane)           -- a

        -- The agents section. Tab moves focus between it and the tree; while
        -- it has focus, Space and Enter jump rather than toggle. See
        -- AgentKeysFollowFocus for what every other key does there.
        SwitchAgentTreeFocus(user, pane)                  -- Tab
        JumpToAgentWindow(user, pane, target)             -- Space, Enter (agents focus)

    @guarantee PanesReadThroughTheBoard
        -- A pane's renderer reads its task and the live agents from the
        -- running board (BoardPaneView) and from nowhere else. It never opens
        -- a connection to the shared store, and never opens the store's
        -- local database for shared rows: the board is the single reader of
        -- shared rows on a machine, as it is the single writer of hook events
        -- (agent-health.allium: HooksWriteThroughTheBoard). Two panes per
        -- agent window, each with a store connection of its own, was a
        -- subscription per pane — and each connection re-delivered every row
        -- the board already held, to show one task and one list.
        --
        -- Read-only, in both directions: the pane asks, the board answers,
        -- and neither the board's rows nor the store change because a pane is
        -- shown (AskingChangesNothing). It does not bend AgentTreeIsGitDerived
        -- either: what the pane says about files still comes from git alone.
        --
        -- A board that cannot be reached is a failed read, handled where every
        -- failed read already is: while the task is awaited, it is waited out
        -- and then named (AgentTreePaneTaskNeverArrives); once the pane is up,
        -- the agents section keeps its last list and says so
        -- (AgentTreeAgentListFailureKeepsLastList). The pane never closes
        -- itself over it. The diff pane holds to the same guarantee
        -- (AgentTreeDiffPaneSaysWhatItIsWaitingFor).

    @guarantee AgentTreePaneSaysWhatItIsWaitingFor
        -- Until its task resolves, the pane draws one line in place of the
        -- tree and the agents section: `waiting for task <id>`. It is never
        -- blank, so a slow store or a restarting board reads as a wait rather than as a broken or
        -- empty pane. In the common case the task resolves at once and the
        -- line is never seen (AgentTreePaneResolvesItsTask).
        --
        -- If the task does not resolve — the wait of
        -- config.agent_tree_startup_wait ran out, or the task has no worktree
        -- — the line is replaced by pane.startup_failure, which stays on
        -- screen for as long as the renderer runs. It is not a notice: it is
        -- not in the border, and no keypress clears it, because there is
        -- nothing behind it to reveal.
        --
        -- While waiting and after giving up, the exit key is the one key
        -- that does anything: it closes the pane, as it always does
        -- (ExitAgentTreeRenderer). Every other key is ignored, since there is
        -- no tree, no agents list and no open set for it to act on. The pane
        -- never closes itself — see "Companion Pane Startup" for the race
        -- that made it do so.

    @guarantee AgentsSectionSitsBelowTheTree
        -- The agents section is a band across the bottom of the pane, under
        -- the tree, with its own titled top border ("Agents (Tab)"). The
        -- "(Tab)" is the only place the pane names the key that reaches the
        -- section, and it reads the same whichever section has focus: Tab is
        -- a toggle, so the one key both enters and leaves. Without it the
        -- section was reachable but undiscoverable (task #4977). It is as tall
        -- as the number of listed agents, capped at
        -- config.agent_tree_agents_max_rows, and the tree keeps every row the
        -- section does not take. Past the cap the section scrolls to keep its
        -- cursor in view. With no agents listed it still shows its border and
        -- one empty row, so Tab always has somewhere visible to go.
        --
        -- The focused section's border is drawn in the focus colour and the
        -- other's in the ordinary border colour, so the user can always see
        -- which section the next key will act on. A notice still reddens the
        -- pane's border (AgentTreeNoticeRedensBorder) — both sections' — and
        -- wins over focus. Its text stays where it always was, on the tree's
        -- bottom border, which now sits directly above the agents section.

    @guarantee AgentRowShowsIdAndTitle
        -- Each agent row reads `#<id> <title>`, clipped at the pane edge like
        -- every other row here. The id comes first because it is the part the
        -- user copies: it is what names the agent's window (`task-<id>`) and
        -- its messaging session.
        --
        -- The pane's own task is marked with a trailing dot and drawn dimmed.
        -- It is listed rather than hidden so the list always reads as the
        -- whole set of running agents, but Space on it does nothing (see
        -- JumpToAgentWindow).
        --
        -- The cursor highlight is drawn only in the focused section. The
        -- unfocused section keeps its cursor position without showing it, so
        -- Tab back returns the user to where they were.

    @guarantee AgentKeysFollowFocus
        -- With the agents section focused, the cursor motions — j/k, Up/Down,
        -- gg, G, Ctrl-D, Ctrl-U — move the agents cursor, with the same clamp
        -- and half-page rules they have in the tree (half a page is half the
        -- section's visible height). h/l and Left/Right do nothing there:
        -- there is nothing to expand. q and Ctrl-C exit the renderer, and `a`
        -- still toggles every file diff, from either section — both act on the
        -- pane as a whole, not on a cursor.
        --
        -- Every key the pane handles, Tab included, still clears a notice
        -- (ClearAgentTreeErrorNotice).

    @guarantee AgentTreeGgChordNeverExpires
        -- `gg` is a two-key chord, and unlike the board's `gg` (core.allium's
        -- config.gg_chord_timeout) it has no time limit. A lone `g` arms the
        -- chord and does nothing else; the chord completes only on a second
        -- `g`, however long the user takes to press it. Any other key
        -- disarms the chord and is then handled normally, exactly as if the
        -- `g` had never been pressed — so a lone `g` is never observable as
        -- anything but a swallowed keystroke.
        --
        -- The board needs a timeout because its idle tick has to reclaim a
        -- stale chord; this pane does not, because `g` is bound to nothing
        -- else here. Nothing is waiting on the chord to expire, so nothing has
        -- to measure how long it has been pending. That keeps the renderer's
        -- key handling a pure function of the keys pressed, with no clock in
        -- it.

    @guarantee MergedDirectoryChainRows
        -- A directory that holds nothing but one other directory gets no row
        -- of its own. The chain is ONE row, named by the whole route:
        -- `src/tui/ui/kanban`, not four rows nested four indents deep. There
        -- is only one thing there to act on, so collapsing that row collapses
        -- the whole chain and the cursor passes over it once.
        --
        -- The row's leading segments are drawn dimmed, its LAST segment in the
        -- ordinary directory colour: the folder the files are actually in
        -- stands out, and the route to it recedes without being hidden. The
        -- route is kept rather than elided because a bare `kanban` is not
        -- something the user can act on — two directories of that name in
        -- different subtrees would be indistinguishable, and neither would say
        -- where to look.
        --
        -- A directory holding a changed file of its own is never merged away,
        -- whatever else it holds. It is not a link in a single-child chain, so
        -- a file's own parent is always the row directly above it.
        --
        -- A merged row is one node for every purpose the pane has: one cursor
        -- stop, one expand, one collapse, and the paths of the files beneath
        -- it are untouched. Merging shortens what is DRAWN, never what is
        -- opened — the path written to the open set for a file under a merged
        -- row is the same path it would have had with every intermediate row
        -- present.

    @guarantee RowsPutAFoldersOwnFilesFirst
        -- Siblings are ordered files first, then directories, each group by a
        -- single name component: a directory's own name, or, for a
        -- chain-merged row, the first segment of its route — never the whole
        -- route as displayed. The third paragraph below is why.
        --
        -- A folder's own changed files therefore sit directly beneath it,
        -- above its subfolders — which is also where the row carrying their
        -- summed counts is, when it carries them
        -- (CountsShowOnTheFolderNearestTheFiles). Interleaved alphabetically,
        -- a file could land after a sibling subtree at a deeper indent than
        -- the row above it, and read as belonging to that subtree rather than
        -- to its own parent.
        --
        -- A directory is weighed by ONE component — its own name, or, for a
        -- chain-merged row, the FIRST segment of its route. A row reading
        -- `agent/tree` sorts where a row reading `agent` would: before the
        -- sibling `agent-health`, rather than after it.
        --
        -- That is why the rule is written this way rather than over the route
        -- as displayed: compressing a chain then never reorders rows. A row
        -- sits where its outermost directory sat, whether or not a sibling
        -- change happens to have split the chain apart this second, so a
        -- refresh that merges or unmerges something does not shuffle the list
        -- under the reader's cursor.
        --
        -- Ordering by the displayed route would forfeit that: a sibling whose
        -- name has the route's first segment as a proper prefix followed by a
        -- byte below `/` (0x2f) would change places as the chain merged and
        -- unmerged, since `agent-health` sorts before `agent/tree` but after
        -- `agent`.
        --
        -- Nothing outside the tree re-derives this order. The diff pane needs
        -- it and is GIVEN it — see AgentTreeDiffPane's Order section for why
        -- it could not compute it for itself.

    @guarantee MergedRoutesClipAtThePaneEdge
        -- A merged route makes a row wider than an unmerged one, and the pane
        -- is only config.agent_tree_pane_percent of the window. A row wider
        -- than that is clipped at the pane's right edge, exactly as a diff
        -- line is (AgentTreeDiffPane's DiffLinesTruncateRatherThanWrap) — not
        -- wrapped, and not elided in the middle.
        --
        -- The cost is stated rather than hidden, because it falls on the wrong
        -- end of the row: what clips first is the LAST segment, the badge and
        -- the counts — the parts MergedDirectoryChainRows says stand out. A
        -- route deep enough to do that is rare, and the user can widen the
        -- pane; see the MergedRouteWidth open question for what else it could
        -- do.

    @guarantee CountsShowOnTheFolderNearestTheFiles
        -- A directory draws its counts on the folder nearest the files, not on
        -- every folder above them. A directory row draws them when every one
        -- of its children is a file — there is no folder further in to carry
        -- them — and leaves them off when it holds another directory, whose
        -- own row carries its own.
        --
        -- The point is that the numbers stop repeating. A file four
        -- directories deep otherwise puts the same `+40 -10` on five rows, and
        -- a pane forty columns wide spends most of its width restating one
        -- fact. Nothing is lost by dropping them: a file row always carries
        -- its own counts, so an ancestor's sum was only ever a summary of rows
        -- already on screen.
        --
        -- The exception is a COLLAPSED directory, which draws its full sum
        -- over everything beneath it however deep. That is the one case where
        -- the sum is not a restatement — the rows it summarises are not on
        -- screen — and it is what makes collapsing a subtree a way to ask how
        -- much is in there rather than a way to lose the answer.
        --
        -- Live expansion state is not modelled in this spec (see the
        -- AgentTreeViewState open question), which is why the collapsed case
        -- is stated here and not as an invariant. On FIRST render the question
        -- does not arise: every directory in the tree is an ancestor of a
        -- change and so is expanded, and the nearest-folder rule is the whole
        -- behaviour. AgentTreeNode.counts holds the sum either way — this
        -- surface decides where it is drawn, never what it is.

    @guarantee AgentTreeIsGitDerived
        -- Everything this surface shows about a file comes from git, and only
        -- from git. The pane does not observe the agent's tool calls, keeps no
        -- log, and remembers nothing across a refresh beyond the last good
        -- answer. There is therefore no state that can disagree with the
        -- repository, and no badge that has to be retracted when the agent
        -- undoes something.
        --
        -- The user-visible shape of that: a file the agent opened but did not
        -- change is not in the tree; a file the agent deleted is in the tree,
        -- badged deleted; a file changed by a shell command is in the tree like
        -- any other. There is no Read badge, because git cannot supply one, and
        -- inventing a second source to supply it is the thing this guarantee
        -- forbids.

    @guarantee AgentTreeBaselineIsTaskBaseBranch
        -- "Changed" always means "changed relative to this worktree's fork
        -- point from the task's base branch" — never relative to HEAD alone,
        -- and never relative to a base tip. Two consequences the user depends
        -- on:
        --
        --   * Committing does not empty the pane. An agent that commits
        --     mid-session still sees its own work, because the baseline is
        --     behind the commit.
        --   * Other people's work on the base branch never appears. The
        --     baseline is the fork point, not the current base tip, so commits
        --     that landed on the base branch after this task branched are not
        --     the agent's changes and are not shown as such.
        --
        -- == Resolving the fork point: a base branch names up to two refs ==
        -- task.base_branch is a NAME, and a repo can hold two refs under it:
        -- the local branch, and its remote-tracking counterpart. They drift
        -- apart in both directions during normal operation, and dispatch
        -- branches a worktree from whichever of the two was ahead at the
        -- moment it provisioned (see dispatch.allium's start-point selection).
        -- So neither ref alone is a safe baseline:
        --
        --   * Local behind remote — the human never checked the base branch
        --     out, or has not pulled it in weeks. The worktree was branched
        --     from the remote ref. Measuring from the local one puts the
        --     baseline before work that was already there when the agent
        --     started, and every upstream commit since is attributed to the
        --     agent.
        --   * Local ahead of remote — a task wrapped up by fast-forwarding the
        --     local base branch without pushing, which is dispatch's own
        --     default. The worktree was branched from the local ref, and
        --     measuring from the remote one mis-attributes in exactly the same
        --     way, only mirrored.
        --
        -- Both errors are the same error and it is the one this guarantee
        -- exists to forbid, so the baseline is resolved against BOTH refs: the
        -- fork point is taken against each ref that resolves, and the one
        -- nearer HEAD wins. A ref that does not resolve is simply not a
        -- candidate — a base branch the human never checked out locally is
        -- ordinary, and must leave the pane working, not failing.
        --
        -- "Fork point" here means the latest common ancestor of HEAD and the
        -- ref — git's merge-base. It does NOT mean git's own `--fork-point`
        -- mode, which consults the reflog and reports nothing at all in a
        -- freshly provisioned worktree that has no reflog to consult.
        --
        -- Nearness is ancestry, not commit count: of two candidates, the one
        -- that has the other as an ancestor is nearer. Two candidates that are
        -- already equal need no ranking and are not compared, which is the
        -- ordinary case. Should neither be an ancestor of the other — possible
        -- only where the two refs have truly diverged — the local ref's fork
        -- point wins. That case has no right answer, since either choice shows
        -- some commits the agent did not write, so it is settled by a fixed
        -- rule rather than a heuristic.
        --
        -- A comparison that cannot be MADE is not the same as one that comes
        -- back "no", and the two must not be conflated. "No" keeps the local
        -- candidate; a comparison git failed to answer — because it could not
        -- run, or overran config.agent_tree_git_timeout — would then keep the
        -- local candidate too, silently reinstating the very mis-attribution
        -- this guarantee forbids and presenting it as a correct tree. So an
        -- unanswerable comparison fails the whole query instead, and the tick
        -- takes AgentTreeGitFailureKeepsLastGoodTree: last good tree, notice,
        -- red border.
        --
        -- When neither ref resolves and the query fails, the message the user
        -- sees is the LOCAL branch's. That is the name they put on the task, so
        -- it is the one they can act on; the remote ref's complaint would name
        -- a ref they never typed.
        --
        -- == The remote is `origin` ==
        -- The remote-tracking counterpart is looked for under `origin`, as it
        -- is everywhere else in dispatch's git handling. A repo whose remote is
        -- named anything else therefore has only one candidate, always, and is
        -- measured from its local base branch exactly as before this resolution
        -- existed — including the mis-attribution when that branch is stale.
        -- The cost is accepted rather than hidden: making the remote name
        -- configurable here alone would leave dispatch's own provisioning,
        -- which picks the start point, still assuming `origin`, so the two
        -- would disagree about what the worktree was branched from — which is
        -- the whole bug. The remote name is one decision for the whole system
        -- or none.
        --
        -- The whole resolution is re-derived from git on every tick and
        -- nothing about it is remembered between refreshes, which is what
        -- keeps AgentTreeIsGitDerived true of the baseline as well as of the
        -- changes. The alternative — recording at provision time which ref the
        -- worktree was branched from — was rejected for that reason: it makes
        -- the pane depend on dispatch-time bookkeeping that no later git
        -- command can confirm or repair, and it says nothing at all about a
        -- worktree that was reused rather than freshly branched.

    @guarantee AgentTreeNoticeRedensBorder
        -- Whenever pane.error_notice is set, the pane's border is drawn in the
        -- error colour, not just the notice text. One rule, every notice — a
        -- failed git query and a failed file open look equally wrong, because
        -- they equally mean "what you are looking at is not what you asked
        -- for".
        --
        -- The border is what makes a stale tree honest. A tree kept on screen
        -- after a failed query (AgentTreeGitFailureKeepsLastGoodTree) is
        -- indistinguishable from a correct one at a glance; a red frame is
        -- visible from across the room, and a single line of border text is
        -- not. Both are shown, and the border is the one that carries.
        --
        -- It clears with the notice, on the next keypress
        -- (ClearAgentTreeErrorNotice).

    @guarantee ReadOnlyObservation
        -- The pane only reads: it runs git queries that inspect the worktree
        -- and change nothing in it. It never writes to the worktree, never
        -- mutates the task, and never issues an MCP call. Killing it, or never
        -- opening it, cannot change what the agent does or what the board
        -- shows.
        --
        -- The git commands this pane runs are read-only by construction — the
        -- baseline resolution's merge-base probes, a diff and a file listing.
        -- Nothing here fetches, commits, stages, checks out or writes to the
        -- index, and an agent's own git operations must never be disturbed by
        -- the pane observing them.
        --
        -- Opening a diff does not breach this and cannot: the diff pane runs
        -- the same read-only git commands this one does, in a pane beside it.
        -- Nothing this feature added can write to the worktree at all, which is
        -- a stronger position than the editor pane it replaced — that one
        -- launched a process that COULD write, at the user's keypress, and
        -- needed a carve-out stated here. The carve-out is gone with it.
        --
        -- One write does exist and it is outside the worktree: the tree records
        -- its open set where the diff pane can read it. It goes in the
        -- worktree's git administrative directory, which git never reports and
        -- which `git worktree remove` deletes with the worktree, so it can
        -- never appear in the tree, in a diff, or in anything the agent might
        -- commit. See OpenSetIsNotWorktreeState.

    @guarantee NoBoardCoupling
        -- The pane is a separate process in a separate window from the board
        -- TUI. It does not appear in the board's App state, does not
        -- participate in the board's DB refresh cycle, and introduces no board
        -- input mode. The board's only involvement is owning the lifetime of
        -- the toggle keybinding.

    @guarantee OpenSetIsNotWorktreeState
        -- The set of files whose diffs are open is the user's, not the
        -- repository's, and it is kept where the repository cannot see it: the
        -- worktree's own git administrative directory. Three properties follow
        -- and all three are required, not merely convenient.
        --
        --   * Git never reports it, so it cannot appear as an untracked file in
        --     the very tree it drives, and the agent can never stage or commit
        --     it.
        --   * `git worktree remove` deletes it with the worktree, so the
        --     teardown that already runs gains no fourth step and no new
        --     failure mode. This is the same placement, chosen for the same
        --     reasons, that the per-task MCP configuration already uses (see
        --     tasks.allium's CallerIdentityConfigGoesWithTheWorktree).
        --   * It is per worktree, so two agents' panes cannot read each
        --     other's.
        --
        -- It is written by the tree and read by the diff pane, in that
        -- direction only. The diff pane never writes it, which is what keeps
        -- the tree the single place a toggle is decided even though two
        -- processes can see the file.
        --
        -- It is not durable state and nothing reads it back after the tree
        -- exits: KillAgentTreeDiffPaneWithItsTree empties the set, so a file
        -- left behind by a killed renderer describes nothing.

    @guarantee OpenDiffPathsMaySurviveTheirFiles
        -- A path stays in the open set even when git stops reporting the file —
        -- the agent reverted it, or committed and reset. It renders nothing
        -- while that lasts, and starts rendering again by itself if the agent
        -- changes the file once more.
        --
        -- Pruning it instead would mean the pane quietly un-asking a question
        -- the user asked, and would make a file the agent is actively editing
        -- flicker in and out of the open set on the AGENT's saves rather than
        -- on the user's keypresses. Only the user closes what the user opened.

    @guidance
        -- The pane's initial state is shown, so an agent window opens with the
        -- tree already visible. Everything the pane displays is derived from a
        -- git query, so it survives its own restart: toggling off and on
        -- rebuilds the same badges, because it asks the same question again.
        --
        -- The cursor position and the user's manual expansions do NOT survive a
        -- restart. They live in the renderer process and are modelled nowhere in
        -- this spec — see the AgentTreeViewState open question. What does survive
        -- a refresh is the manual expansion state within one renderer's life: a
        -- directory the user collapsed stays collapsed when new changes land.
        --
        -- The open set is view state of the same kind and gets the same
        -- treatment: KillAgentTreeDiffPaneWithItsTree empties it when the tree
        -- goes, so toggling the pane off and on gives a bare tree. It is
        -- modelled in this spec, unlike the cursor, only because a second
        -- process has to read it.
}

surface AgentTreeDiffPane {
    facing user: User

    -- One surface instance per diff pane. The user reading it is looking at
    -- the pane beneath the tree, in the agent's own tmux window.
    context pane: AgentTreePane

    exposes:
        pane.open_diff_paths
        pane.root
        pane.diff_task_resolution
        pane.diff_startup_failure when pane.diff_task_resolution = unresolved

    contracts:
        demands BoardPaneView

    provides:
        -- Keys handled by the diff renderer itself, unprefixed, while the diff
        -- pane has tmux focus. Deliberately the same vocabulary the tree uses
        -- for the same motions, so moving between the two panes does not mean
        -- changing keyboards. Every one but the exit key acts on the view.
        ExitAgentTreeDiffRenderer(user, pane)               -- q, Ctrl-C
        ScrollAgentTreeDiffUp(user, pane)                   -- k, Up
        ScrollAgentTreeDiffDown(user, pane)                 -- j, Down
        ScrollAgentTreeDiffHalfPageUp(user, pane)           -- Ctrl-U
        ScrollAgentTreeDiffHalfPageDown(user, pane)         -- Ctrl-D
        JumpAgentTreeDiffToTop(user, pane)                  -- gg
        JumpAgentTreeDiffToBottom(user, pane)               -- G

    @guarantee AgentTreeDiffPaneSaysWhatItIsWaitingFor
        -- The diff pane's form of AgentTreePaneSaysWhatItIsWaitingFor, and
        -- the same behaviour exactly: `waiting for task <id>` until its own
        -- read of the task resolves, then either the diffs or
        -- pane.diff_startup_failure, which stays until the pane closes. Only
        -- the exit key acts meanwhile, and the pane never closes itself.
        --
        -- Its wait is its own. A tree that resolved long ago does not make a
        -- freshly split diff pane skip the wait, and a diff pane that gives
        -- up leaves the tree above it untouched.
        --
        -- Its read is the board's too: it asks the running board for its
        -- task and opens no store connection, exactly as the tree does
        -- (AgentTreeCompanionPane: PanesReadThroughTheBoard).

    @guarantee DiffPaneHasNoToggleOfItsOwn
        -- The diff pane cannot open or close a file's diff. Space, Enter and
        -- the all-files key do nothing here; the open set is decided in the
        -- tree and only in the tree.
        --
        -- That is what makes one file the user is looking at have one open
        -- state. Two places to toggle from would mean two writers to the open
        -- set across two processes, and the tree's row markers could disagree
        -- with the pane beneath them — which NodeDiffOpenMatchesOpenSet
        -- forbids and this arrangement makes unreachable rather than merely
        -- illegal.

    @guarantee DiffPaneNavigationIsTmuxNavigation
        -- Moving the cursor between the tree and the diff is tmux's own pane
        -- navigation — the prefix and a direction — and this spec binds
        -- nothing for it. Neither pane draws a focus indicator, because tmux
        -- already draws the active pane's border differently, and neither
        -- renderer needs to know whether it has focus.
        --
        -- This is why the diff went into a second pane rather than a second
        -- region inside the tree's pane. A region would have needed a focus
        -- model, a focus indicator, a key to move between the two, and a rule
        -- for which region each existing motion key acted on. A pane needs
        -- none of those, and it can be resized and zoomed with the tmux the
        -- user already knows.

    @guarantee DiffLinesTruncateRatherThanWrap
        -- A diff line wider than the pane is cut at the pane's edge. It is not
        -- wrapped, and the rest is not shown on a continuation row.
        --
        -- Wrapping is the tempting alternative and it is worse here, because
        -- the pane is narrow by design (config.agent_tree_pane_percent): one
        -- long line would wrap into many rows and push several files' diffs off
        -- screen, so the cost of a single minified or generated line would be
        -- paid by everything the user opened alongside it. Truncation costs
        -- only the line it happens to. See the DiffPaneWidth open question.

    @guarantee DiffPaneIsGitDerivedToo
        -- Everything this pane shows comes from git, exactly as the tree's
        -- badges do (AgentTreeIsGitDerived). It reads the open set to know
        -- WHICH files to ask about, and nothing else: the paths come from the
        -- tree, the contents never do. So the two panes cannot disagree about
        -- what changed, and a diff cannot outlive the fact behind it — the next
        -- tick asks again.
        --
        -- It writes nothing at all, not even the open set (see
        -- DiffPaneHasNoToggleOfItsOwn), which makes it strictly more read-only
        -- than the tree that spawned it.

    @guidance
        -- The pane scrolls as ONE document: every open file's diff, in tree
        -- order, under its own path heading. It is not one scroll region per
        -- file and there is no per-file cursor, so scrolling past the end of
        -- one file reaches the top of the next without a keystroke in between.
        --
        -- Its scroll position lives in the renderer process and is not modelled
        -- here, the same way the tree's cursor is not — see the
        -- AgentTreeViewState open question, which covers both.

}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "AgentTreeViewState: the renderer's cursor position and the set of directories the user has manually expanded or collapsed are real, observable state — CollapseSelectedAgentTreeNode and friends act on them, DirectoryExpansionFollowsChangedDescendants only constrains the first render, not what the user does afterwards, and CountsShowOnTheFolderNearestTheFiles now READS live expansion to decide whether a directory row draws its sum. They are currently modelled nowhere. Should AgentTreePane carry them as fields (making the manual-override behaviour rule-expressible and the invariant honest about when it applies), or is per-process view state deliberately below this spec's abstraction line?"

open question "MergedRouteWidth: a chain-merged directory row carries its whole route, so a deep one can be wider than the pane — around forty columns — and MergedRoutesClipAtThePaneEdge says it is then clipped from the right, losing the last segment, the badge and the counts. Those are the parts of the row the merging was meant to make prominent, so clipping is the least useful place to lose width. Should a route too wide for the pane elide its middle segments instead, keeping the first and last, should the row drop leading segments until it fits, or is clipping plus the user's own tmux resize the intended answer here as it is for the diff pane?"

open question "DiffPaneWidth: the diff pane inherits the companion pane's column, which is config.agent_tree_pane_percent of the window — around forty columns on a typical terminal. Diff lines wider than that are truncated at the pane edge (AgentTreeDiffPane's DiffLinesTruncateRatherThanWrap), which is legible for most source but loses the right-hand side of long lines outright, with no way to reach it from inside the pane. Should the pane offer horizontal scrolling, should opening a diff widen the column, or is truncation plus the user's own tmux resize the intended answer?"

open question "IgnoredFileChanges: git's ignore rules decide what the pane can see, so an agent writing to an ignored path (a build artefact, a scratch file, a local config) leaves no trace in the tree at all. That is usually right — an ignored file is ignored for a reason — but it does mean the pane cannot show an agent scribbling somewhere it should not. Is silent omission the intended behaviour, or should ignored changes be surfaced somewhere distinguishable?"
